/* SRE & Cloud Deployment Foundations — lessons for M05–M08.

   Same shape as part 1: objective, prerequisites, 8–20 minute reading
   estimate, a mental model, 2–6 sections, one worked example, misconceptions,
   a short recall/prediction exercise (answer before commands), and references
   into the source register (LX.sreSources ids). Every example is Beacon
   (Python HTTP API + worker + PostgreSQL) on invented hosts (corp.lab,
   registry.lab, looped-foundations). Tool behaviour follows the pinned
   versions: Terraform S3 backend uses use_lockfile and DynamoDB locking is
   deprecated; Kubernetes apps/v1; image tags are pinned, never a floating
   tag. */
window.LX = window.LX || { commands: [], scenarios: [], drills: [] };
LX.sreLessons = LX.sreLessons || [];

/* ═══ M05 · Cloud architecture and access ═══════════════════════════ */
LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m05-01', moduleId: 'M05', title: 'The AWS request path', mins: 14,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m05-01'], prereqs: ['les-m01-01', 'les-m03-05'], refs: ['T08'],
  objective: 'Trace a sensor record from a client through DNS, the load balancer, the VPC, the security groups and the application to the database, and name at which hop each class of failure would first show.',
  model: 'A request to Beacon in AWS crosses a fixed sequence of **hops**, and every hop has its own way of saying no. **DNS** turns a name into addresses. The **load balancer** (ALB for HTTP, NLB for raw TCP) accepts the connection and forwards to a **target** that passed its health check. The **VPC** decides by **route table** whether packets can reach the target subnet at all, then the **security group** (stateful, allow-only, on the ENI) and the **network ACL** (stateless, on the subnet) decide whether they are admitted. Only then does Beacon run, and Beacon itself then becomes a client to PostgreSQL and to AWS APIs, where **IAM** decides. The failure tells you the hop: `NXDOMAIN` is DNS, a timeout is routing or a filter, `503` from the ALB is "no healthy target", and `AccessDenied` is identity.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'Hop by hop: the data plane', body: 'Start at the client. `dig api.beacon.corp.lab` returns the ALB\'s addresses (an alias record, one address per AZ the ALB lives in). The client opens TCP 443 to one of them; the ALB terminates TLS with its certificate, reads the HTTP request, picks a **target group** and sends a new connection to a healthy target on the target\'s port (8080 for Beacon). That second connection is the one the VPC sees: source = the ALB\'s node in its subnet, destination = the Beacon task\'s private IP.\n\nFor the packet to arrive, the ALB subnet\'s route table must have a route to the target subnet (inside one VPC the `local` route always does), the target\'s security group must allow inbound 8080 **from the ALB\'s security group**, and the subnet NACLs on both sides must allow it, including the return traffic on ephemeral ports because NACLs are stateless. Beacon then opens its own connection to PostgreSQL on 5432, where the database security group must allow inbound from Beacon\'s security group. Each of these is a separate object with a separate owner, which is why "the network" is never one thing.' },
    { h: 'Security groups versus network ACLs', body: 'A **security group** is attached to a network interface, is stateful (a reply to an allowed request is allowed automatically), has only allow rules, and evaluates all rules together — there is no ordering. You can reference another security group as the source, which is how "allow 8080 from the ALB" is written without hard-coding addresses. A **network ACL** is attached to a subnet, is stateless (you must allow the reply direction explicitly, typically ephemeral ports 1024–65535), has numbered allow and deny rules evaluated lowest number first, and applies to every interface in the subnet.\n\nThe operational consequence: almost all day-to-day access control lives in security groups; NACLs are a coarse, rarely changed boundary, and a default NACL allows everything. When something times out, check the security group first because it is specific; when a whole subnet cannot talk, suspect a NACL or a route. Both produce **timeouts**, never refusals — a filter silently drops, so `Connection refused` means the packet arrived and nothing was listening.' },
    { h: 'ALB versus NLB, and what their errors mean', body: 'An **Application Load Balancer** works at HTTP: it terminates TLS, routes on host and path, sets `X-Forwarded-For`, and returns its own status codes when it cannot complete a request. A **503** with no Beacon log line means no target in the group was healthy; a **502** means a target closed the connection or sent a malformed response; a **504** means the target did not answer within the idle timeout. A **Network Load Balancer** works at TCP (or TLS passthrough): it forwards bytes, preserves the client source address for instance targets, offers static addresses, and cannot produce an HTTP error — a dead target shows as a connection reset or a timeout at the client.\n\nBoth depend on **health checks** against each target. The check path must be one Beacon serves on the target port without authentication; a health check on a path that returns 404 marks every target unhealthy and the ALB returns 503 for everyone while every Beacon process is running happily. Deciding ALB or NLB is deciding where you want errors to be visible and whether you need HTTP routing.' },
    { h: 'Control plane versus data plane', body: 'Everything above is the **data plane**: packets moving between things that already exist. The **control plane** is the set of API calls that create and change those things: `CreateLoadBalancer`, `AuthorizeSecurityGroupIngress`, `RunTask`. Control-plane calls are authenticated with IAM and can fail with `AccessDenied` or throttling; data-plane traffic does not consult IAM at all, which is why a request can be denied by a security group while every IAM policy is perfect, and why `aws elbv2 describe-target-health` (a control-plane read) is how you learn what the data plane is doing to your targets.\n\nKeeping the two apart tells you which tool to reach for. A client timeout is a data-plane symptom: look at routes, security groups, health checks. A deploy that fails to create a task is a control-plane symptom: look at IAM and service quotas. The two share nothing but names.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'A sensor POST that returns 503, hop by hop', body: 'Ticket: "POST https://api.beacon.corp.lab/v1/status returns 503 since the 10:00 deploy."\n\n- DNS: `dig +short api.beacon.corp.lab` returns two addresses. Not DNS.\n- ALB: `curl -si https://api.beacon.corp.lab/healthz` returns `503 Service Unavailable` with header `Server: awselb/2.0`. The ALB answered, so TLS and routing to the ALB work; the ALB had no healthy target.\n- Targets: `aws elbv2 describe-target-health --target-group-arn <tg>` shows both targets `unhealthy`, reason `Target.ResponseCodeMismatch`, description `Health checks failed with these codes: [404]`.\n- Hypotheses: H1 the new build dropped `/healthz`; H2 the target group\'s health path was changed; H3 the security group blocks the ALB (would be `Target.Timeout`, not a 404, so H3 is out).\n- Discriminating check: `aws elbv2 describe-target-groups` shows `HealthCheckPath: /health` — changed in the deploy\'s Terraform from `/healthz`. Beacon serves `/healthz`.\n- Smallest change: restore the health check path. Within one interval the targets report `healthy` and the user path `curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}" -X POST .../v1/status` returns `202`.\n\nThe 503 was never about Beacon crashing; it was the ALB telling you that its view of the targets was empty.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'A timeout means the service is down.', right: 'A timeout means a packet was dropped somewhere: a route, a security group or a NACL. A down service with an open path gives `Connection refused` almost instantly.' },
    { wrong: 'Security groups are evaluated in order like a firewall rule list.', right: 'Security group rules are a set of allows with no ordering and no deny; NACLs are the ordered, numbered, allow-or-deny list, and they are stateless.' },
    { wrong: 'IAM is involved in every request to Beacon.', right: 'IAM authorises control-plane API calls and calls Beacon makes to AWS services. Client traffic through the ALB to Beacon never consults IAM.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict: the ALB target group\'s health check path returns 404 on every target. What does a client see, and is any Beacon process unhealthy?', a: 'The ALB returns 503 for every request because it has no healthy target. Every Beacon process may be running fine; the health check path is wrong.' },
    { q: 'Beacon\'s security group allows inbound 8080 from the ALB security group. The NACL on Beacon\'s subnet allows inbound 8080 but has no outbound rule for ephemeral ports. What happens?', a: 'The SYN arrives but the reply is dropped by the stateless NACL; the ALB sees a timeout and marks the target unhealthy.' },
    { q: 'Which hop produced the error: `curl: (6) Could not resolve host: api.beacon.corp.lab`?', a: 'DNS, before any packet reached AWS. Check the record and the resolver; nothing downstream has been tested yet.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m05-02', moduleId: 'M05', title: 'IAM, STS and KMS: who is allowed what', mins: 16,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m05-01', 'obj-m05-02'], prereqs: ['les-m05-01'], refs: ['T18', 'T08'],
  objective: 'Predict allow or deny for a request against a set of IAM, resource and KMS policies the way the service evaluates them, explain why a role session stops working at a specific time, and propose a least-privilege narrowing that keeps other boundaries intact.',
  model: 'IAM answers one question for every API call: **is this principal allowed this action on this resource under these conditions?** The answer starts as **implicit deny**. An **explicit deny** anywhere wins over everything. Otherwise the request must be allowed by every boundary that applies — organisation **SCP**, **permission boundary**, **session policy** — and by an **identity policy** on the caller or a **resource policy** on the target. **STS** hands out temporary credentials for a **role session** with a fixed expiry; after that moment every call fails with `ExpiredToken` no matter what the policies say. **KMS** is a resource with its own **key policy** that is the root of authority for the key; IAM policies only work for a key if the key policy lets them, and services use **grants** to act on your behalf.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'The evaluation order, as a procedure', body: 'When a request arrives, the service collects every policy that could apply and runs this order:\n\n1. Is there an **explicit `Deny`** that matches (action, resource, condition) in any policy — identity, resource, SCP, boundary, session? If yes: denied, stop.\n2. Does an **SCP** on the account allow the action? If the account is in an organisation and no SCP allows it: denied.\n3. Does a **resource-based policy** on the target allow this principal? In the same account, a resource-policy allow is sufficient on its own for an IAM user or role; cross-account, both sides must allow.\n4. Does the **permission boundary** (if the principal has one) allow it? A boundary never grants; it caps.\n5. Does the **session policy** (if the role was assumed with one) allow it? Same rule: it caps.\n6. Does an **identity-based policy** attached to the user, group or role allow it?\n\nIf no allow survives the caps, the result is the default: implicit deny. The practical reading of a fixture is therefore: look for a matching Deny first, then look for a matching Allow, then check that nothing caps it. `NotAction` and `NotResource` in a Deny are the classic surprise: `"Effect": "Deny", "NotAction": "s3:*"` denies everything that is not S3, including the STS call you need.' },
    { h: 'Roles, STS and why it worked an hour ago', body: 'Beacon does not hold long-lived keys. Its task runs with a **role**; the runtime calls `sts:AssumeRole` and receives an access key, a secret, a **session token** and an **Expiration**. The role\'s trust policy says who may assume it (a service principal, another role, an identity provider); the role\'s permission policies say what the session may do. `aws sts get-caller-identity` shows which identity your credentials carry — an ARN like `arn:aws:sts::123456789012:assumed-role/beacon-worker/task-1` means a session, not the role itself.\n\nSessions expire. The default is one hour; a role\'s `MaxSessionDuration` can allow up to twelve, and role chaining (assuming a role from a role) is capped at one hour. SDKs refresh automatically when they obtained the credentials themselves, but credentials copied into a shell or a config file do not refresh. The error is unmistakable once you have seen it: `ExpiredToken: The security token included in the request is expired`. That is not a permission problem; no policy change fixes it. The fix is a new session, and the design fix is to let the SDK own the credentials.' },
    { h: 'KMS: key policies, grants and the error that looks like S3', body: 'A KMS key has a **key policy** attached to the key itself. It is the only policy that always applies, and if it does not include a statement letting IAM policies in the account control the key (the usual `"Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:root"}` with `kms:*`), then no IAM policy in the account can grant access to that key at all. Encrypting an object with SSE-KMS needs `kms:GenerateDataKey` on the key; reading it needs `kms:Decrypt`. Services that encrypt on your behalf (EBS, RDS, S3 with bucket keys) use **grants**, time-limited permissions that a principal creates for a service with `kms:CreateGrant`, so a `kms:ViaService` condition is how you say "this key may only be used through S3 in this region".\n\nThe symptom that catches people is where the error appears. A worker with `s3:GetObject` but no `kms:Decrypt` gets a `403 AccessDenied` from **S3**, because S3 is the service it called; the S3 error message names KMS but the role\'s S3 permissions are fine. The discriminating check is the key: `aws kms describe-key --key-id <arn>` to find it, then its policy and the role\'s policy for `kms:Decrypt` on that ARN.' },
    { h: 'Narrowing without breaking the neighbours', body: 'Least privilege means the policy allows what the workload has been observed to do, on the resources it does it to, and nothing else. To narrow safely you need the observed set: CloudTrail events for the role, or a trial period with a broad policy and `iam:GenerateServiceLastAccessedDetails`. Then write the Allow with specific actions (`s3:PutObject`, not `s3:*`), specific resources (`arn:aws:s3:::beacon-ingest-looped-foundations/raw/*`, not `*`) and conditions where they add safety (`aws:SourceVpce` for a VPC endpoint, `s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption`).\n\nThe trap is narrowing in a way that silently removes a boundary someone else relies on. Deleting a `Deny` statement to make your Allow work widens access for every principal that statement covered. Replacing a permission boundary rather than editing within it removes a cap. The rule: a least-privilege change touches only the Allow for the principal in question and is tested with the service\'s own simulator (`aws iam simulate-principal-policy`) against the request that must work and the request that must still be denied.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'Predicting the answer for a worker upload', body: 'Fixture: the worker role\'s identity policy allows `s3:PutObject` on `arn:aws:s3:::beacon-ingest-looped-foundations/*`. The bucket policy has `"Effect": "Deny", "Action": "s3:PutObject", "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::beacon-ingest-looped-foundations/*", "Condition": {"StringNotEquals": {"s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption": "aws:kms"}}`. The bucket\'s default encryption is SSE-KMS with key `beacon-ingest`. The role has no `kms:` permissions. The request: `PutObject raw/2026-10-13/sensor-0042.json` with no encryption header.\n\n- Step 1, explicit deny: the bucket policy Deny matches — the header is absent, so `StringNotEquals` is true. **Denied**, regardless of the Allow. Error: `AccessDenied` from S3.\n- Change the request to send `--server-side-encryption aws:kms`. Step 1 passes. Step 3 and 6: the identity Allow matches. Allowed by IAM — but S3 must now call KMS as the worker: `kms:GenerateDataKey` on `beacon-ingest`. The role has none. **Denied again**, still reported as `AccessDenied` by S3, message mentioning KMS.\n- Narrowing fix: add to the worker role an Allow for `kms:GenerateDataKey` on that one key ARN with `"kms:ViaService": "s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com"`. Do not touch the bucket Deny; it is what guarantees nothing lands unencrypted.\n\nThe prediction was made from the policy text alone; `simulate-principal-policy` then confirms allow for the upload and deny for an unencrypted one.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'If my identity policy allows it, the call is allowed.', right: 'An explicit deny in any applicable policy wins, SCPs and boundaries can cap, and KMS adds its own key policy. The allow is necessary, not sufficient.' },
    { wrong: '`ExpiredToken` means the role lost a permission.', right: 'It means the session\'s fixed expiry has passed. Policies were not consulted; get a new session and let the SDK refresh credentials.' },
    { wrong: 'A `403 AccessDenied` from S3 is always an S3 permission problem.', right: 'With SSE-KMS the denial is often the KMS key: `kms:GenerateDataKey` to write, `kms:Decrypt` to read, and the key policy must admit IAM at all.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'A role has `s3:*` allowed on `*`. An SCP on the account denies `s3:DeleteBucket`. Predict the result of `DeleteBucket`.', a: 'Denied: the explicit deny in the SCP wins over any identity allow.' },
    { q: 'Predict the first thing to check when a worker that worked for 59 minutes starts failing every AWS call at once.', a: 'Session expiry: look for `ExpiredToken` in the error and at how the credentials were obtained; a policy change would not hit every call at the same instant.' },
    { q: 'Which policy must allow access for a KMS key to be usable through IAM at all?', a: 'The key policy. Without a statement that admits the account\'s IAM principals, identity policies have no effect on that key.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m05-03', moduleId: 'M05', title: 'Regions, AZs, subnets, routes and endpoints', mins: 16,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m05-03'], prereqs: ['les-m03-01', 'les-m03-04', 'les-m05-01'], refs: ['T08'],
  objective: 'Lay out a VPC across two availability zones with public and private subnets, route tables, NAT and VPC endpoints so Beacon\'s private tasks reach PostgreSQL, S3 and AWS APIs without a public address, and justify each route.',
  model: 'A **region** is a set of independent **availability zones** (AZs), each its own power and network failure domain. A **VPC** is a private address block in one region; a **subnet** is a slice of it pinned to one AZ. What a subnet can reach is decided by its **route table**: a route to an **internet gateway** makes it "public"; a route to a **NAT gateway** lets private subnets start outbound connections without accepting inbound; a **gateway endpoint** (S3, DynamoDB) is a route-table entry that keeps traffic to those services inside AWS; an **interface endpoint** is a network interface in your subnet with a private address that answers for a service\'s hostname. Lay out every subnet twice, once per AZ, and you have removed one AZ from the list of things that can take Beacon down.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'Subnets are AZ-bound; route tables decide public or private', body: 'A VPC `10.40.0.0/16` in a region with AZs `use1-az1` and `use1-az2` (AZ ids are stable across accounts; names like `us-east-1a` are shuffled per account) gets at least four subnets: `10.40.0.0/24` public in az1, `10.40.1.0/24` public in az2, `10.40.10.0/24` private in az1, `10.40.11.0/24` private in az2. "Public" is not a property of the subnet; it is the presence of a `0.0.0.0/0 → igw-…` route in the table associated with it, plus a public address on the interface. A private subnet\'s table has `10.40.0.0/16 → local` (always, cannot be removed) and, if egress is needed, `0.0.0.0/0 → nat-…`.\n\nThe ALB lives in the public subnets, one node per AZ. Beacon tasks and the database live in private subnets and never get a public address, so nothing on the internet can open a connection to them even if a security group were misconfigured. That layering — route, then filter — is what "defence in depth" means in a VPC.' },
    { h: 'NAT: outbound only, and per AZ', body: 'A **NAT gateway** sits in a public subnet with an elastic IP; a private subnet routed to it can start connections to the internet (pulling an image from `registry.lab` over the public path, calling a third-party API) and receive the replies, but nothing can start a connection inward. It is charged per hour and per gigabyte processed, and it is bound to the AZ of its subnet. Routing the az2 private subnet through a NAT in az1 works until az1 fails, at which point az2 loses egress too — a quiet way to make two AZs into one failure domain. The pattern is one NAT per AZ and one private route table per AZ pointing at its own NAT.\n\nAsk whether egress is needed at all. If the only outbound destinations are AWS services, endpoints replace the NAT entirely, which removes a cost, a dependency and an exposure.' },
    { h: 'Gateway endpoints versus interface endpoints', body: 'A **gateway endpoint** exists for S3 and DynamoDB. It adds a route (destination: the service\'s managed prefix list `pl-…`, target: `vpce-…`) to the route tables you choose; traffic to S3 from those subnets then never leaves the AWS network and never needs a NAT. It costs nothing, has no network interface and no security group, and supports an **endpoint policy** that limits which buckets or actions may be used through it.\n\nAn **interface endpoint** (PrivateLink) exists for most other services — STS, KMS, ECR, CloudWatch Logs, Secrets Manager. It creates a network interface with a private address in each subnet you select, protected by a security group that must allow 443 from your workloads, and with **private DNS** enabled the service\'s public hostname (`kms.us-east-1.amazonaws.com`) resolves inside the VPC to that private address, so clients need no configuration. It is charged per hour per AZ and per gigabyte, so it is a deliberate choice per service, and like the NAT it must be placed in both AZs or it becomes a cross-AZ dependency.\n\nThe diagnostic that tells you which path traffic takes is `dig kms.us-east-1.amazonaws.com` from inside the VPC: a `10.40.x.x` answer means the interface endpoint; a public address means NAT or nothing.' },
    { h: 'Reading a layout for exposure and dependency', body: 'For every component ask three questions. **How does traffic reach it** — which route in which table, which security group rule? **How does it reach what it needs** — the database by `local` route, S3 by gateway endpoint, STS and KMS by interface endpoints or NAT, the registry by NAT? **What AZ does it depend on** — its own subnet, its NAT, its endpoints, the database primary? Write the answers down; the inventory lesson later in this module turns them into a machine-readable file.\n\nTwo layouts that look identical on a diagram differ in these answers. A single NAT and single-AZ endpoints give "two AZs" on paper and one in practice. A `0.0.0.0/0 → igw` route on the database subnet gives "private" in the name and public in fact. The plan is the drawing of routes, not the drawing of boxes.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'Giving the worker private access to S3 and KMS', body: 'Requirement: the Beacon worker in the private subnets uploads to `beacon-ingest-looped-foundations` (SSE-KMS) and must have no public address and no internet egress.\n\n- S3: create a gateway endpoint for `com.amazonaws.us-east-1.s3`, associate the two private route tables. The tables gain `pl-63a5400a → vpce-0a1b…`. Endpoint policy: allow only the ingest bucket.\n- KMS and STS: create interface endpoints for `com.amazonaws.us-east-1.kms` and `com.amazonaws.us-east-1.sts` in both private subnets, private DNS on, security group allowing 443 from the worker security group.\n- Remove the `0.0.0.0/0 → nat-…` route from the private tables. Delete the NAT gateways once nothing else needs them.\n- Verify from a worker task: `dig +short kms.us-east-1.amazonaws.com` returns `10.40.10.23` and `10.40.11.41`; `aws s3 cp sensor-0042.json s3://beacon-ingest-looped-foundations/raw/` succeeds; `curl -m 5 https://registry.lab` times out, which is the intended proof that there is no egress.\n\nThe image pull now needs its own path (ECR interface endpoints, or pulling in a public subnet at build time), and that becomes a named dependency rather than an accident of the NAT.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'A subnet is public because it is named "public".', right: 'Public means its route table sends `0.0.0.0/0` to an internet gateway and the interface has a public address. Read the route table, not the tag.' },
    { wrong: 'One NAT gateway serves the whole VPC safely.', right: 'It serves it, but every private subnet routed through it depends on that NAT\'s AZ. One per AZ with per-AZ route tables keeps the failure domains separate.' },
    { wrong: 'Interface and gateway endpoints are the same thing with different names.', right: 'Gateway endpoints are route entries for S3 and DynamoDB, free, no ENI. Interface endpoints are ENIs with private DNS and a security group for most other services, charged per hour and GB.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict: from a private subnet with a gateway endpoint for S3 and no NAT, what happens to `aws sts get-caller-identity`?', a: 'It times out. The gateway endpoint covers S3 only; STS needs an interface endpoint or a NAT route.' },
    { q: 'Both private subnets route `0.0.0.0/0` to a NAT gateway in the az1 public subnet. az1 fails. Which Beacon components keep working?', a: 'Tasks in az2 keep serving traffic that needs no egress, but anything in az2 that needs the internet (registry pulls, third-party calls) fails with the NAT.' },
    { q: 'How do you prove, from inside the VPC, that KMS traffic is using the interface endpoint?', a: 'Resolve `kms.<region>.amazonaws.com`; an address inside the VPC CIDR means private DNS is pointing at the endpoint ENI.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m05-04', moduleId: 'M05', title: 'Compute and storage choices', mins: 14,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m05-04'], prereqs: ['les-m05-01', 'les-m05-03'], refs: ['T08'],
  objective: 'Choose a compute and a storage option for each Beacon component by stating the requirement, the managed-versus-self-managed tradeoff, the stateless-versus-stateful consequence and the cost, so a reviewer can disagree with a reason.',
  model: 'Every component is either **stateless** (any instance can serve any request; killing one loses nothing) or **stateful** (it owns data that must survive). Stateless things are cheap to run on anything that restarts them — a container service, a function — and scale by adding copies. Stateful things need storage with a known **durability** and **failure domain**: a block volume lives in one AZ, an object store spans a region, a managed database replicates for you if you pay for it. **Managed** means the provider runs the control loop (patching, failover, backups) and you give up some configuration and pay a premium; **self-managed** means you keep every knob and every 3 a.m. page. The choice is a requirement matched to a tradeoff, written down.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'Compute: how much of the machine do you want to own?', body: 'The options are a spectrum of ownership. **EC2** gives you a VM: you own the OS, patching, the process manager, the agent, and you can run anything. **ECS on Fargate** (or any managed container runtime) gives you a container with CPU and memory you declare: no host, no kernel patching, but no privileged access and a per-second price above raw EC2. **Lambda** gives you a function invoked per event with a duration cap, scaled to zero when idle, priced per invocation and millisecond — ideal for bursty, short work, wrong for a long-running listener. **EKS** gives you Kubernetes with the control plane managed and the nodes yours or Fargate\'s; it is the right answer when you already need Kubernetes semantics, not a default.\n\nBeacon\'s API is a long-lived HTTP listener with steady load: a container service with two tasks across two AZs behind the ALB. The worker drains a queue continuously: the same. A nightly export that runs for four minutes is a Lambda candidate. Picking EC2 for all three "because we know it" is a legitimate choice only if the patching and host failover are then written into the recovery plan as your work.' },
    { h: 'Storage: durability and failure domain first', body: '**EBS** is a block device attached to one instance in one AZ; it is fast, it is where a self-managed PostgreSQL would put its data, and it dies with its AZ unless you snapshot it (snapshots go to S3, regional). **EFS** is a shared POSIX filesystem across AZs for files that several tasks must read and write, at a latency cost. **S3** is an object store with eleven nines of durability across AZs in a region, no filesystem semantics, cheap, and the right home for sensor payloads, exports and backups. **RDS** is a managed PostgreSQL: storage, patching, automated backups, point-in-time recovery, and with **Multi-AZ** a synchronous standby in another AZ that takes over in one to two minutes via a DNS change.\n\nThe question for each dataset is: how much can we lose (RPO) and how long can we be without it (RTO)? Sensor records already in S3 are safe once written. The PostgreSQL state is the thing that needs a decision, and the decision is between RDS Multi-AZ (managed, failover built in, about double the single-AZ price) and a self-managed pair on EC2 with EBS where you build and test the failover yourself.' },
    { h: 'Stateless by design, so compute stays cheap', body: 'A component is only stateless if it holds nothing a user would miss: no sessions in memory, no files written to its own disk that anyone reads later, no in-process queue of unsent work. Beacon\'s API accepts a record, writes it to PostgreSQL (or S3) and returns `202`; if the task dies between accepting and writing, the client retries with the same idempotency key and nothing is lost. That property lets the container service replace tasks freely, lets the ALB spread load across AZs and lets scaling be a number.\n\nThe worker is where state creeps in: a batch half-processed in memory, a temp file on the task\'s ephemeral disk. Make the queue the owner of the work (visibility timeout, acknowledge after commit) and the worker is stateless again. Every time you move state out of compute into storage that already has a durability story, the compute choice gets simpler and cheaper.' },
    { h: 'Writing the tradeoff so someone can disagree', body: 'A choice that cannot be argued with is not a design decision; it is a habit. Write each one as four lines: the requirement in numbers (`p95 under 300 ms`, `RPO 5 min`, `no public address`), the option chosen, the option rejected and what it would have given, and the cost in money and in operational work. "RDS Multi-AZ, because failover in under two minutes without an on-call runbook is worth roughly 2x the storage-tier price; rejected self-managed on EC2 because we would own patching and would have to test failover quarterly" is a line a reviewer can accept or attack.\n\nThe same discipline catches overspend. Multi-AZ RDS for a dev environment that is rebuilt from a seed every morning is paying for a durability nobody asked for; a single instance with a nightly snapshot states its RPO honestly at 24 hours and costs half.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'The Beacon component table', body: 'The design worksheet for D01 came out as:\n\n```\ncomponent    state      compute            storage           why\nbeacon-api   stateless  ECS Fargate x2/AZ  none              HTTP listener, steady load; tasks replaceable; no host to patch\nbeacon-worker stateless ECS Fargate x1/AZ  none (queue owns)  continuous drain; ack after commit keeps it stateless\nbeacon-db    stateful   RDS PostgreSQL     Multi-AZ, gp3      RPO ~0 with sync standby, RTO 1-2 min, PITR 7 days; 2x cost accepted\nraw payloads stateful   -                  S3 SSE-KMS        regional durability, lifecycle to cheaper tier after 30 days\nexport job   stateless  Lambda nightly     writes to S3      4-minute run, idle 23h; EC2 rejected as always-on waste\n```\n\nThe rejected alternative for `beacon-db` was PostgreSQL on an EC2 pair with EBS and streaming replication: cheaper per month, but failover would be a runbook we have to write, test and be paged for, and EBS snapshots give an RPO measured in hours unless we also run WAL archiving. The team chose to buy that work from the provider and wrote the price next to the choice.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'Managed always means better.', right: 'Managed means the provider runs the control loop and you pay for it and lose some knobs. For a workload rebuilt daily, or one that needs a feature the managed option lacks, self-managed can be the honest choice.' },
    { wrong: 'A container is stateless because it is a container.', right: 'Statelessness is about where data lives. A container writing results to its own filesystem is stateful and loses them on replacement.' },
    { wrong: 'EBS volumes are safe because AWS is durable.', right: 'An EBS volume lives in one AZ. Durability across AZs comes from snapshots, replication or using S3/RDS Multi-AZ.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict what is lost if a self-managed PostgreSQL on a single EBS volume in az1 loses its AZ, with nightly snapshots.', a: 'Up to a day of writes (RPO 24 h) and the time to restore the snapshot into another AZ (RTO hours), unless WAL archiving was also set up.' },
    { q: 'Name the one property that lets the ALB and container service replace Beacon API tasks at will.', a: 'The API holds no state a user would miss: every accepted record is durably written before `202`, and clients retry with an idempotency key.' },
    { q: 'What four things does a written compute or storage decision contain so a reviewer can disagree?', a: 'The requirement in numbers, the option chosen, the option rejected and what it offered, and the cost in money and operational work.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m05-05', moduleId: 'M05', title: 'Dependencies, failure domains and recovery', mins: 15,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m05-05', 'obj-m05-03'], prereqs: ['les-m05-03', 'les-m05-04'], refs: ['T08'],
  objective: 'Produce a machine-readable inventory of everything Beacon depends on, mark each single point of failure and its failure domain, and write a recovery plan with a stated RPO and RTO that someone else could execute.',
  model: 'A **dependency** is anything whose failure changes what a user sees. A **failure domain** is the set of things that fail together: one AZ, one region, one account, one deploy, one vendor. A **single point of failure** is a dependency that lives in exactly one failure domain with no stand-in. **RPO** (recovery point objective) is how much data you accept losing, measured backwards from the failure; **RTO** (recovery time objective) is how long you accept being down, measured forwards. A recovery plan is the ordered list of actions that meets those two numbers, and it is only real once it has been run. Two AZs remove one failure domain from your list; they do nothing about a bad deploy, a corrupted table, an expired certificate or a region.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'Inventory first, in a file a program can read', body: 'Memory is not an inventory. Write one as YAML and keep it in the repo, because the next person will grep it during an incident:\n\n```\ndependencies:\n  - id: beacon-db\n    kind: rds-postgres\n    domain: [az:use1-az1, az:use1-az2]   # Multi-AZ\n    spof: false\n    rpo: 0s\n    rto: 120s\n    owner: platform\n  - id: ingest-bucket\n    kind: s3\n    domain: [region:us-east-1]\n    spof: true          # region-bound; accepted, see recovery.md\n  - id: registry.lab\n    kind: external-registry\n    domain: [vendor:registry.lab]\n    spof: true          # image pulls at scale-out fail if down\n  - id: cert-api-beacon\n    kind: tls-certificate\n    expires: 2027-01-14\n    spof: true\n```\n\nEvery row answers: what is it, which domain does it fail with, is there a stand-in, who owns it. Include the quiet ones: the DNS zone, the certificate, the NAT gateways, the IAM role whose trust policy someone could edit, the queue. A dependency you forgot to list is one you will discover at the worst time.' },
    { h: 'Failure domains and what two AZs do and do not buy', body: 'Lay the inventory over the domains. Everything with a single AZ in its `domain` list is a candidate SPOF: a NAT gateway in az1 only, a self-managed database on one EBS volume, an interface endpoint placed in one subnet. Spreading those across two AZs with per-AZ routing means the loss of one AZ costs capacity, not availability.\n\nNow list what two AZs do not touch. A **region** outage takes both. A **deploy** that ships a broken image goes to both AZs at once, which is why rollouts are gradual and roll back on a health signal (M08, M10). A **data** fault — a migration that drops a column, a worker that writes garbage — is replicated synchronously to the standby in under a second, so Multi-AZ faithfully preserves the corruption; only point-in-time recovery or a backup from before the fault helps, and that is a restore with an RTO of its own. An **account** or **credential** fault (a deleted role, an expired certificate, a revoked key) is global. A **vendor** fault, like the registry, is outside your account entirely. Each of these gets its own line in the plan or an explicit "accepted, not mitigated".' },
    { h: 'RPO, RTO and the mechanisms that deliver them', body: 'State the numbers per dataset, then pick the mechanism that can honestly deliver them. **Synchronous replication** (RDS Multi-AZ) gives RPO near zero and RTO of one to two minutes for an AZ failure, and nothing for a logical error. **Point-in-time recovery** from continuous backups gives RPO of about five minutes to any moment in the retention window, with an RTO of however long a restore of that size takes — measure it, it is often an hour. **Snapshots** give RPO equal to their interval. **Cross-region copies** of snapshots and a documented rebuild give a region story with an RTO measured in hours.\n\nThe plan is written as steps against a specific failure: "AZ use1-az1 lost: 1. confirm RDS failover completed (`aws rds describe-events`), 2. confirm the ALB has healthy targets in az2, 3. scale the az2 service to the full count, 4. check the NAT in az2 carries egress, 5. verify the user path". A second plan for "ingest table corrupted at 09:40": "1. stop the worker, 2. restore to 09:35 as a new instance, 3. verify row counts, 4. repoint, 5. replay the queue". Each step names a command and what success looks like.' },
    { h: 'A plan is a claim until it has been executed', body: 'The first time a recovery plan runs should not be during the outage. Run it in a scheduled exercise against the staging environment: time each step, note the step that needed a credential nobody had, record the real RTO next to the target. The exercise output is evidence; the plan without it is a hope with headings. M14 turns this into a routine.\n\nWhen the measured RTO misses the target, you have two honest moves: change the mechanism (a warm standby instead of a restore) and pay for it, or change the target and tell the people who depend on it. The dishonest move is to leave the target in the document and the mechanism in the wrong tier, which is what most "we have DR" claims turn out to be.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'Finding the SPOF that the diagram hid', body: 'The Beacon layout had two AZs everywhere: two public subnets, two private subnets, an ALB in both, Fargate tasks in both, RDS Multi-AZ. The inventory exercise added the quiet rows and one came up red:\n\n```\n  - id: nat-egress\n    kind: nat-gateway\n    domain: [az:use1-az1]\n    spof: true\n    used_by: [beacon-worker, beacon-api]   # registry pulls, third-party geo API\n```\n\nBoth private route tables pointed at the single NAT. The AZ-loss plan was rehearsed by blackholing the az1 NAT route in staging: az2 tasks kept serving existing traffic, but the first scale-out event could not pull the image from `registry.lab` and the worker\'s geo lookups timed out, so new sensor records were accepted and then stuck. Measured RTO for "full service in az2": 40 minutes of manual route edits against a target of 5.\n\nTwo changes: a second NAT in az2 with its own route table, and ECR interface endpoints so image pulls need no NAT at all. The row became `domain: [az:use1-az1, az:use1-az2], spof: false`, and the re-run of the exercise met the target with no manual steps. The diagram had not changed; the inventory had.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'Multi-AZ means we have disaster recovery.', right: 'It covers an AZ failure. A bad deploy, a data corruption, an expired certificate or a region loss hits both AZs; each needs its own mechanism or an explicit acceptance.' },
    { wrong: 'A synchronous standby protects the data.', right: 'It protects against losing the instance. A destructive write is replicated faithfully; only point-in-time recovery or a pre-fault backup protects the data.' },
    { wrong: 'A written recovery plan satisfies the RTO.', right: 'Only a rehearsed plan with a measured time does. Until it has been run, the RTO is a guess.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict: a migration drops the `readings` table at 09:40 on an RDS Multi-AZ instance. Does failover help? What does?', a: 'No; the standby received the same drop within a second. Point-in-time recovery to 09:39 into a new instance, verify, repoint, and replay the queue.' },
    { q: 'Name three dependencies that are easy to leave out of an inventory and are single points of failure by default.', a: 'A TLS certificate with an expiry, a single NAT gateway, and an external registry or vendor API (also: the DNS zone, an IAM role trust policy).' },
    { q: 'How is an RTO number turned from a claim into evidence?', a: 'By executing the plan in an exercise, timing each step, and recording the measured time next to the target; then changing mechanism or target if it misses.' }
  ]
});

/* ═══ M06 · Terraform and desired state ═════════════════════════════ */
LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m06-01', moduleId: 'M06', title: 'HCL: providers, resources, data, variables, outputs', mins: 15,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m06-01'], prereqs: ['les-m01-03'], refs: ['T17'],
  objective: 'Write a Terraform configuration with a pinned provider, resources and data sources that reference each other, validated input variables, locals and outputs, and have it pass `terraform fmt -check` and `terraform validate`.',
  model: 'A Terraform configuration is a description of **what should exist**, not a script of what to do. Each **resource** block names one object a **provider** knows how to create, read, update and delete; a **data** block reads something that already exists; **variables** are the inputs, **locals** are named expressions, **outputs** are what the configuration tells the outside world. References between blocks (`docker_image.beacon.image_id`) are not just values — they are edges in a **dependency graph** that Terraform builds before doing anything, and the graph decides order. Write the blocks; Terraform works out the sequence.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'The blocks and what each one is for', body: 'A minimal, honest configuration for Beacon on the Docker provider:\n\n```\nterraform {\n  required_version = ">= 1.10"\n  required_providers {\n    docker = { source = "kreuzwerker/docker", version = "~> 3.0" }\n  }\n}\n\nprovider "docker" {}\n\nresource "docker_image" "beacon" {\n  name = "registry.lab/beacon/api:${var.beacon_version}"\n}\n\nresource "docker_container" "api" {\n  name  = "beacon-api"\n  image = docker_image.beacon.image_id\n  env   = ["BEACON_DB_URL=${local.db_url}"]\n  ports {\n    internal = 8080\n    external = var.api_port\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`terraform` pins the Terraform and provider versions; `provider` configures how to talk to the thing (here the local Docker socket); each `resource` has a type (`docker_container`), a local name (`api`) and arguments. The **address** `docker_container.api` is how every other block, the plan and the state refer to it. Rename the local name and Terraform sees a different object (the `moved` block in lesson 05 is the cure).' },
    { h: 'Data sources: read, never create', body: 'A `data` block asks the provider for something that exists already and exposes its attributes: `data "docker_network" "lab" { name = "beacon-net" }` reads a network someone else created; `data "aws_caller_identity" "me" {}` tells you the account you are in. Data sources are read during plan when their arguments are known, so `data.docker_network.lab.id` can be used in a resource argument and still show a concrete value in the plan. If a data source\'s arguments depend on a resource that does not exist yet, Terraform defers the read to apply and shows `(known after apply)`.\n\nUse data sources for anything owned elsewhere — a shared network, an AMI id, a VPC created by another configuration. Managing it as a `resource` in two places is the classic way to get two state files fighting over one object.' },
    { h: 'Variables with validation, locals, outputs', body: 'A variable declares an input with a type, a description and optionally a default and a validation rule:\n\n```\nvariable "beacon_version" {\n  type        = string\n  description = "Image tag to deploy, e.g. 1.4.2 (never a floating tag)"\n  validation {\n    condition     = can(regex("^[0-9]+\\\\.[0-9]+\\\\.[0-9]+$", var.beacon_version))\n    error_message = "beacon_version must be a semantic version like 1.4.2."\n  }\n}\n\nvariable "api_port" {\n  type    = number\n  default = 8080\n  validation {\n    condition     = var.api_port > 1024 && var.api_port < 65536\n    error_message = "api_port must be an unprivileged port."\n  }\n}\n\nlocals {\n  db_url = "postgres://beacon@${docker_container.db.name}:5432/beacon"\n}\n\noutput "api_endpoint" {\n  value = "http://127.0.0.1:${var.api_port}"\n}\n```\n\nValidation fails at plan time with your message, before any provider is contacted — a `latest` tag or port 80 never reaches the plan. Locals are named expressions, evaluated once, for anything used twice. Outputs are the published interface: another configuration reads them via remote state or a human reads them with `terraform output -raw api_endpoint`. Values come from `terraform.tfvars`, `-var`, or `TF_VAR_beacon_version` in the environment.' },
    { h: 'The dependency graph and depends_on', body: 'Every reference is an edge. `docker_container.api` references `docker_image.beacon.image_id`, so the image is pulled first; it references `docker_container.db.name` through the local, so the database container is created first. Terraform creates everything with no unmet edges in parallel, which is why two unrelated resources appear in the output interleaved. `terraform graph` prints the graph; reading it is how you check that order is what you believe.\n\n`depends_on = [docker_container.db]` adds an edge where no attribute reference exists — useful when the dependency is behavioural (the API needs the database to be listening) rather than data. Use it sparingly: an explicit edge the data does not justify hides the real reason for ordering, and it forces the dependent resource to be deferred (`known after apply`) more often. Prefer a real reference when one exists.\n\nBefore committing: `terraform fmt -check` (canonical layout, so diffs are only meaning) and `terraform validate` (types, references, required arguments, variable validation). Both run without credentials or a backend and belong in the pipeline.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'From "it works" to a configuration that passes review', body: 'First draft: a single `docker_container` with `image = "registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.2"` hard-coded, the database URL typed in, no outputs. It applied. Review asked three questions: how do we deploy 1.4.3, how does the worker get the same database URL, what port is it on?\n\nSecond draft: `variable "beacon_version"` with the semver validation; `local.db_url` used by both `docker_container.api` and `docker_container.worker`; `output "api_endpoint"`. Then the checks:\n\n```\n$ terraform fmt -check\nmain.tf\n$ terraform fmt && terraform validate\nSuccess! The configuration is valid.\n$ terraform plan -var beacon_version=latest\n│ Error: Invalid value for variable\n│   beacon_version must be a semantic version like 1.4.2.\n$ terraform plan -var beacon_version=1.4.3\n  # docker_image.beacon will be updated in-place ...\n```\n\n`fmt -check` listed the file it would change (exit code 3), so the pipeline would have stopped; after formatting, `validate` passed; the validation rule rejected the floating tag before any provider was contacted; and a real version produced a plan. The configuration now says what Beacon should be, in a form a reviewer can read and a pipeline can check.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'Terraform runs the blocks top to bottom like a script.', right: 'It builds a dependency graph from references and applies in graph order, in parallel where nothing is connected. Block order in the file is irrelevant.' },
    { wrong: 'A data source creates the thing if it is missing.', right: 'A data source only reads. A missing object is an error at plan time, which is the point: it belongs to someone else.' },
    { wrong: 'Variable validation runs during apply.', right: 'It runs at plan (and validate), before any provider call, so a bad input never produces a partial apply.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict: `docker_container.api` uses `docker_image.beacon.image_id`. Which is created first, and did you have to say so?', a: 'The image; the reference is an implicit edge in the graph, so no `depends_on` is needed.' },
    { q: 'A `variable "api_port"` has a validation requiring a value above 1024. What does `terraform plan -var api_port=80` print, and does it contact the provider?', a: 'An `Invalid value for variable` error with your `error_message`; the provider is never contacted.' },
    { q: 'Two configurations both declare `resource "docker_network" "lab"` for the same network. What is the correct shape?', a: 'One owns it as a resource; the other reads it with a `data "docker_network"` block. Two owners mean two states fighting over one object.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m06-02', moduleId: 'M06', title: 'plan and apply: reading what will change', mins: 14,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m06-02', 'obj-m06-01'], prereqs: ['les-m06-01'], refs: ['T17'],
  objective: 'Read a Terraform plan line by line, predict which objects will be created, updated in place, destroyed or replaced and what data each action puts at risk, and explain what the provider lockfile pins.',
  model: 'A **plan** is Terraform\'s diff between three things: the **configuration** (what you wrote), the **state** (what it believes exists) and the **real objects** (refreshed from the provider at plan time). Each resource gets a verdict marked by a symbol: `+` create, `~` update in place, `-` destroy, `-/+` destroy then create (**replace**), `+/-` create then destroy, `<=` read during apply. The summary line counts them. `apply` executes exactly a saved plan or re-plans and asks. Reading the plan is the skill; applying is clicking a button. The **lockfile** `.terraform.lock.hcl` is a separate promise: it pins the provider versions and checksums so a plan on your laptop and in the pipeline are computed by the same code.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'The symbols and the summary line', body: 'Run `terraform plan` after changing the image tag and the restart policy:\n\n```\nTerraform will perform the following actions:\n\n  # docker_container.api must be replaced\n-/+ resource "docker_container" "api" {\n      ~ id      = "7f3c9a..." -> (known after apply)\n      ~ image   = "sha256:aa11..." -> "sha256:bb22..." # forces replacement\n        name    = "beacon-api"\n      ~ restart = "no" -> "unless-stopped"\n        # (12 unchanged attributes hidden)\n    }\n\n  # docker_image.beacon will be updated in-place\n  ~ resource "docker_image" "beacon" {\n      ~ name = "registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.2" -> "registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.3"\n        id   = "sha256:aa11...registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.2"\n    }\n\nPlan: 1 to add, 1 to change, 1 to destroy.\n```\n\nRead the header line of each block first: `must be replaced` versus `will be updated in-place` is the whole story. Then find the attribute carrying `# forces replacement` — that is the one that caused it. `(known after apply)` means the value is decided by the provider during apply (an id, an address). `(12 unchanged attributes hidden)` is reassurance that only the shown lines differ. The summary counts a replacement as one add and one destroy, so `1 to add, 1 to destroy` with no new resource in the configuration is a replacement in disguise.' },
    { h: 'What each action does to data', body: 'An **update in place** calls the provider\'s update; the object keeps its identity and whatever it holds. A **create** is new. A **destroy** removes the object and anything stored only in it: a container\'s writable layer, an instance\'s ephemeral disk, a database with no snapshot. A **replace** is a destroy followed by a create (`-/+`), so for a container the writable layer is gone and the new one starts empty — fine for the stateless API, catastrophic for a PostgreSQL container whose data lives in the container rather than a volume. `+/-` (create before destroy, from `lifecycle { create_before_destroy = true }`) keeps the old object alive until the new one exists, which avoids downtime but requires that both can exist at once (two containers cannot share one name or one published port).\n\nThe plan does not know what is inside the object; you do. For every `-` and `-/+`, write what data is in it and where else that data lives. That annotation is the deliverable of this objective, and it is what you bring to the replacement lesson.' },
    { h: 'Order, parallelism and what apply actually runs', body: 'Apply walks the graph: destroys happen in reverse dependency order, creates in forward order, and independent branches run in parallel (default ten at once). A replacement of the database container forces anything that references its attributes to be re-evaluated, which can turn a one-line change into a cascade — the plan shows the cascade, which is why you read the whole thing and not just the block you touched.\n\n`terraform plan -out=beacon.tfplan` saves the plan; `terraform apply beacon.tfplan` executes exactly that plan with no re-evaluation and no prompt. If the world changed between plan and apply (someone edited the container by hand), the saved plan is stale and apply fails rather than doing something unplanned. In a pipeline the saved plan is the reviewed artefact: the reviewer approves the file, not a re-run that might differ.\n\n`terraform apply` with no file re-plans and asks `Do you want to perform these actions?`; `-auto-approve` removes the question and belongs only where a human has already approved the saved plan.' },
    { h: 'The provider lockfile', body: '`terraform init` resolves `required_providers` constraints to exact versions and writes `.terraform.lock.hcl`:\n\n```\nprovider "registry.terraform.io/kreuzwerker/docker" {\n  version     = "3.0.2"\n  constraints = "~> 3.0"\n  hashes = [\n    "h1:...",\n    "zh:...",\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nCommit it. With the file present, `init` on any machine installs that exact version and verifies the checksum, so the same plan is produced everywhere. Without it, the pipeline silently picks up a newer `3.0.x` whose behaviour differs, and "it planned clean on my machine" becomes a real sentence. `terraform init -upgrade` is the deliberate act of moving the pin forward; review the lockfile diff like code. If the pipeline runs on a different platform (`linux_amd64` versus your `darwin_arm64`), `terraform providers lock -platform=linux_amd64 -platform=darwin_arm64` records hashes for both so neither side rejects the file.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'Annotating a plan before the apply review', body: 'The change under review moved the Beacon database to a named volume and bumped the API image. The plan:\n\n```\n  # docker_container.db must be replaced\n-/+ resource "docker_container" "db" {\n      ~ id    = "c41e..." -> (known after apply)\n        image = "sha256:9d2f..."\n        name  = "beacon-db"\n      + volumes { # forces replacement\n          + container_path = "/var/lib/postgresql/data"\n          + volume_name    = "beacon-pgdata"\n        }\n    }\n  # docker_volume.pgdata will be created\n  + resource "docker_volume" "pgdata" {\n      + name = "beacon-pgdata"\n    }\n  # docker_container.api must be replaced\n-/+ resource "docker_container" "api" {\n      ~ image = "sha256:aa11..." -> "sha256:bb22..." # forces replacement\n    }\n\nPlan: 3 to add, 0 to change, 2 to destroy.\n```\n\nAnnotation submitted with the review:\n\n- `docker_volume.pgdata` create: new, empty, no risk.\n- `docker_container.db` replace, forced by the new `volumes` block: the **current** container holds PostgreSQL data in its writable layer with no volume. Destroying it loses every row. The new container starts with an empty volume. **Do not apply as is**: dump first (`pg_dump` to a file outside the container), apply, restore into the new container, verify row counts.\n- `docker_container.api` replace, forced by `image`: stateless, in-flight requests lost for the seconds of replacement; acceptable.\n\nThe plan said `2 to destroy`; only the annotation said which destroy mattered.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: '`1 to add, 1 to destroy` means one new thing and one removed thing.', right: 'It is also exactly what a replacement looks like. Read each block\'s header for `must be replaced` and find the `# forces replacement` attribute.' },
    { wrong: 'The lockfile is a cache and should be ignored by Git.', right: 'It pins provider versions and checksums so every machine computes the same plan. Commit it; move it forward deliberately with `init -upgrade`.' },
    { wrong: 'Apply re-reads the configuration, so the plan is just a preview.', right: 'A saved plan file is executed exactly and fails if reality drifted. Without a file, apply re-plans and asks; the reviewed artefact should be the file.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict the summary line for a configuration where only `docker_container.api`\'s `image` changes, and `image` forces replacement.', a: '`Plan: 1 to add, 0 to change, 1 to destroy.` — a replacement counts as one add and one destroy.' },
    { q: 'What does `(known after apply)` next to `id` tell you, and is it a problem?', a: 'The provider assigns the value during apply. It is normal for ids and addresses of new or replaced objects.' },
    { q: 'The pipeline on Linux fails `init` with a lockfile checksum error after you ran `init` on an ARM Mac. What is the fix that keeps the pin?', a: '`terraform providers lock -platform=linux_amd64 -platform=darwin_arm64` to record hashes for both platforms, then commit the lockfile.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m06-03', moduleId: 'M06', title: 'State, locking, drift and import', mins: 18,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m06-03', 'obj-m06-04'], prereqs: ['les-m06-02'], refs: ['T03', 'T17'],
  objective: 'Explain what the state file maps and why it must be locked, decide whether a held lock is active or stale using the lock metadata, detect out-of-band drift with a refresh-only plan, and bring an existing object under management with an import block until the plan shows no changes.',
  model: 'The **state** is Terraform\'s memory: a mapping from each resource address (`docker_container.db`) to the real object\'s id and last-known attributes. Configuration says what should exist; state says what Terraform thinks does; the provider says what actually does. **Drift** is the gap between state and reality, made by anyone changing the object outside Terraform. A **backend** stores the state somewhere shared (the S3 backend, with `use_lockfile = true`); a **lock** prevents two runs from reading and writing it at once. **Import** teaches the state about an object that exists but that it has never seen. The whole lifecycle is about keeping the three in agreement, and every command in this lesson moves one of them toward the others.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'What state holds and where it lives', body: '`terraform state list` prints addresses; `terraform state show docker_container.db` prints what the state remembers about one of them — the id the provider assigned, every attribute as of the last apply or refresh. That id is the only link between the address in your configuration and the container on the host; lose the state and Terraform will plan to create everything again while the old objects keep running.\n\nState therefore lives in a **backend** that is shared, versioned and locked. The S3 backend:\n\n```\nterraform {\n  backend "s3" {\n    bucket       = "looped-foundations-tfstate"\n    key          = "beacon/staging/terraform.tfstate"\n    region       = "us-east-1"\n    encrypt      = true\n    use_lockfile = true\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`use_lockfile = true` makes Terraform take the lock as an object next to the state (`terraform.tfstate.tflock`) using conditional writes, so no second service is needed. The older `dynamodb_table` argument is **deprecated**: it still works for now, but new configurations should not add a DynamoDB table for locking, and existing ones should migrate to `use_lockfile`. Bucket versioning is the other half: every state write is a recoverable version. State contains secrets the providers returned (passwords, tokens), so the bucket is encrypted and access to it is as restricted as access to the things it describes.' },
    { h: 'Locks: active or stale, and the only honest way to tell', body: 'Every plan and apply takes the lock first. A second run meanwhile fails:\n\n```\n│ Error: Error acquiring the state lock\n│ Lock Info:\n│   ID:        9c1f2e4a-...\n│   Path:      looped-foundations-tfstate/beacon/staging/terraform.tfstate\n│   Operation: OperationTypeApply\n│   Who:       ana@build-07.corp.lab\n│   Created:   2026-10-13 09:41:18 UTC\n```\n\nThe decision is not "how long has it been held" but "is the holder still running". `Who` names a user and host; `Created` gives the start; `Operation` says apply, which can legitimately take many minutes. The honest check is to find the process: ask Ana, look at build-07\'s job log, check the pipeline run. A lock that is held by a job still writing is **active**, and breaking it corrupts the state the moment both runs write. A lock whose holder crashed, was killed or whose host is gone is **stale**, and `terraform force-unlock 9c1f2e4a-...` with that exact ID is the correct and only repair.\n\nNever force-unlock to make your own run go faster, never do it from a guess about elapsed time, and record what you found when you do it. A lock from a pipeline that failed mid-apply also means the state may be behind reality; the next step after unlocking is a refresh-only plan, not an apply.' },
    { h: 'Drift: seeing it without accepting it', body: 'Someone ran `docker update --restart=always beacon-api` by hand during an incident. State says `restart = "unless-stopped"`; reality says `always`. Plain `terraform plan` refreshes first and shows a change back to the configured value — it would revert the hand edit. To see the drift on its own, without any proposal to change it:\n\n```\n$ terraform plan -refresh-only\nNote: Objects have changed outside of Terraform\n\n  # docker_container.api has changed\n  ~ resource "docker_container" "api" {\n      ~ restart = "unless-stopped" -> "always"\n    }\n```\n\nNow you choose. If the hand edit was wrong, the normal `plan` and `apply` restore the configuration. If it was right, update the configuration to `restart = "always"` and the next plan shows no change. `terraform apply -refresh-only` is the third option: accept reality into the state without touching the object, used when the configuration already matches or the attribute is not managed. What you never do is leave drift unexamined, because the next unrelated apply will silently revert it.' },
    { h: 'Import: adopting what already exists', body: 'The `beacon-net` network was created by hand before Terraform arrived. Managing it means telling the state its id. Write the resource block, then an import block:\n\n```\nimport {\n  to = docker_network.lab\n  id = "beacon-net"\n}\n\nresource "docker_network" "lab" {\n  name   = "beacon-net"\n  driver = "bridge"\n}\n```\n\n`terraform plan` shows `docker_network.lab will be imported` plus any attribute differences between the block and the real object; `apply` records it. If you do not want to hand-write the block, `terraform plan -generate-config-out=generated.tf` writes one from the real object for you to trim. The older imperative form `terraform import docker_network.lab beacon-net` does the same without a block in code and without a plan to review; it still works, but the block is reviewable and repeatable, so prefer it. The goal state for either is the same: a following `terraform plan` that prints `No changes.` If it still proposes a change, the block does not describe the object yet; fix the block, not the object.\n\nThe mirror operations: `terraform state rm docker_network.lab` forgets an object without destroying it (it keeps running, unmanaged), and `terraform state mv` renames an address in state — though the `moved` block in lesson 05 does that in code, with a plan.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'A failed pipeline, a held lock and a drifted container', body: 'Monday 09:52. `terraform plan` in staging fails with the lock error above: `Who: ci@runner-12`, `Created: 09:41`, `Operation: OperationTypeApply`. Elapsed eleven minutes is not evidence either way.\n\n- Discriminating check: the pipeline UI shows run #418 on runner-12 ended at 09:44 with `exit 137` (killed, out of memory) during apply. The holder is dead: stale lock.\n- `terraform force-unlock 9c1f2e4a-...` — ID copied from the error, recorded in the incident note with the run number.\n- Because the apply died mid-way, state may be behind reality: `terraform plan -refresh-only` reports `docker_container.worker has changed: env` — the run had recreated the worker with the new `BEACON_QUEUE` variable before it died, so the object is new but the state still holds the old container id and env.\n- Reality matches the intended configuration, so `terraform apply -refresh-only` accepts it into state. A plain `terraform plan` then prints `No changes. Your infrastructure matches the configuration.`\n\nState, configuration and reality agree again, and nothing was destroyed to get there. The alternative — force-unlock and `apply` straight away — would have planned to create a worker container whose name already existed and failed half-way for a second time.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'The S3 backend needs a DynamoDB table to lock state.', right: 'Set `use_lockfile = true`; the lock is an object beside the state. The `dynamodb_table` argument is deprecated and should not be added to new configurations.' },
    { wrong: 'A lock older than a few minutes is stale and safe to break.', right: 'Staleness is about whether the holder is still running, which you verify from the `Who` and the job or process, not from elapsed time. Breaking an active lock corrupts state.' },
    { wrong: 'Drift is fixed by running apply.', right: 'Apply reverts the drift to the configuration, which may be the wrong direction. A refresh-only plan shows the drift first; then you choose configuration, reality, or accepting into state.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict what `terraform plan` proposes after someone deleted a managed container by hand, and what `plan -refresh-only` shows instead.', a: 'Plain plan: `+` create the container again. Refresh-only: the object `has been deleted` outside Terraform, with no proposal to act.' },
    { q: 'A lock error shows `Operation: OperationTypeApply, Who: ci@runner-12, Created: 09:41`. What one fact decides whether to force-unlock?', a: 'Whether that run is still executing. Check the pipeline job for runner-12; only a dead holder makes the lock stale.' },
    { q: 'After an import, `terraform plan` still shows `~ driver = "bridge" -> "overlay"`. Which side do you change?', a: 'The configuration block, to describe the real object; the plan is telling you the block is not yet an accurate description.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m06-04', moduleId: 'M06', title: 'Replacement and immutable fields', mins: 14,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m06-02', 'obj-m06-03'], prereqs: ['les-m06-02', 'les-m06-03'], refs: ['T17'],
  objective: 'Explain why a provider marks an attribute as forcing replacement, recognise `-/+` and `+/-` in a plan and what each does to the object\'s data, protect stateful resources with lifecycle rules, and choose a safe path (volume, dump and restore, targeted replace) instead of applying a destructive plan.',
  model: 'Some attributes of a real object cannot be changed after creation: a container\'s image, an instance\'s AMI, a volume\'s AZ, a database\'s engine. The provider declares these **ForceNew**; when the configuration changes one, the only way to reach the desired state is to destroy the object and create a new one — a **replacement**, shown as `-/+` with `# forces replacement` on the attribute. Replacement is correct for stateless things and a data-loss event for stateful ones. **Lifecycle** rules let you refuse (`prevent_destroy`), reorder (`create_before_destroy`) or ignore (`ignore_changes`); `-replace` lets you force one on purpose. The skill is to see the replacement coming in the plan and to have moved the data somewhere a replacement cannot reach.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'Why some attributes force replacement', body: 'A provider maps each attribute to what its API can do. Docker can change a running container\'s restart policy (`docker update`), so `restart` updates in place. It cannot change the image a container was created from; the container **is** that image plus a writable layer. So the Docker provider marks `image` (and `name`, and the `ports` and `volumes` blocks, among others) as forcing a new object. The same logic gives AWS its list: an EC2 `ami`, an RDS `engine`, a subnet\'s `availability_zone`. The provider documentation marks them, and the plan annotates them: `~ image = "sha256:aa11..." -> "sha256:bb22..." # forces replacement`.\n\nThis is not Terraform being destructive; it is Terraform being honest about what the API allows. The design response is to make the objects that get replaced hold nothing: an image change replaces the API container (fine), and the data lives in a `docker_volume` that is a separate resource with no changed attributes (untouched).' },
    { h: '-/+ versus +/-, and lifecycle rules', body: 'Default replacement is **destroy then create** (`-/+`): the old container stops and is removed, then the new one starts. For a service that means a gap. `lifecycle { create_before_destroy = true }` flips it to `+/-`: create the new object first, then destroy the old — no gap, but both must be able to coexist, so a fixed `name` or a published host port makes it impossible (two containers cannot bind the same host port), and the provider will error during apply.\n\n`lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true }` on the database container and the volume turns any plan that would destroy them into an error:\n\n```\n│ Error: Instance cannot be destroyed\n│   on main.tf line 40:\n│   40: resource "docker_volume" "pgdata" {\n│ Resource docker_volume.pgdata has lifecycle.prevent_destroy set, but the plan\n│ calls for this resource to be destroyed.\n```\n\nThat error is the guard rail doing its job: it is removed deliberately, in a reviewed change, when the destruction is intended. `ignore_changes = [env]` tells Terraform to stop comparing an attribute — correct for values some other system legitimately owns (an autoscaler\'s desired count), dangerous as a way to make a plan quiet.' },
    { h: 'Forcing a replacement on purpose', body: 'Sometimes the object is the problem: a container whose filesystem was corrupted, an instance that will not boot. The configuration has not changed, so the plan is empty. `terraform apply -replace=docker_container.api` plans a replacement of exactly that address and nothing else, and shows it before asking:\n\n```\n  # docker_container.api will be replaced, as requested\n-/+ resource "docker_container" "api" {\n```\n\nThe older `terraform taint` marked the object in state for replacement on the next apply; it is superseded by `-replace`, which is explicit in the plan and leaves no hidden mark in state. `-target=` narrows an apply to one address and its dependencies; it is an escape hatch for recovering from a half-applied run, not a routine way to work, because it leaves the rest of the configuration unapplied and the summary unreviewed.' },
    { h: 'The safe path when the plan wants to destroy data', body: 'When a plan shows `-/+` on something stateful, stop and choose one of three paths. **Move the data out of the object first**: add a `docker_volume`, dump the database, apply, restore, verify counts; from then on the container is replaceable and the volume is protected. **Change the configuration so the attribute does not change**: if the replacement is caused by a cosmetic rename, a `moved` block or keeping the name avoids it entirely. **Accept the loss deliberately**: for a dev database rebuilt from a seed, replacement is the fastest reset, and the plan annotation says so.\n\nWhat is never the path: applying because the pipeline was green, or adding `ignore_changes` to hide the attribute. The plan was the warning; the annotation and the chosen path are the evidence that it was read.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'The database container that was about to be replaced', body: 'Week 17, lab L12. The change bumped PostgreSQL from `16.4` to `16.6` by editing `docker_image.pg.name`. Plan:\n\n```\n  # docker_container.db must be replaced\n-/+ resource "docker_container" "db" {\n      ~ image = "sha256:9d2f..." -> "sha256:e07b..." # forces replacement\n        name  = "beacon-db"\n        volumes {\n            container_path = "/var/lib/postgresql/data"\n            volume_name    = "beacon-pgdata"\n        }\n    }\nPlan: 1 to add, 0 to change, 1 to destroy.\n```\n\nReasoning: `image` forces replacement; the data directory is on `docker_volume.pgdata`, which is **not** in the plan, so the rows survive the container\'s destruction. The new container mounts the same volume and PostgreSQL 16.6 reads a 16.x data directory without a dump. The risk is downtime during `-/+` and the state of the volume if the old container is killed mid-write; the plan was applied after `pg_dump` to `backups/pre-16.6.sql` as insurance and a check that `prevent_destroy` was set on the volume.\n\nAfter apply: `docker exec beacon-db psql -U beacon -c "select count(*) from readings"` matched the pre-change count, and a second `terraform plan` printed `No changes.` Had the volume not existed, the correct answer was the volume migration first and this upgrade second — never this plan as written.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'Terraform replaces things because it is careless with state.', right: 'The provider marks attributes the API cannot change after creation; replacement is the only way to reach the new value. The plan tells you exactly which attribute did it.' },
    { wrong: '`create_before_destroy` is always the safer choice.', right: 'It needs both objects to coexist. A fixed name or a published host port makes the create fail; use it where identities are unique per object.' },
    { wrong: '`ignore_changes` is a good way to stop a noisy plan.', right: 'It hides a real difference. It is correct only for attributes another system legitimately owns; otherwise fix the configuration or the object.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict: the plan shows `-/+` on `docker_container.db` and no entry for `docker_volume.pgdata`. Is the data at risk?', a: 'Not from this plan: the volume is untouched and the new container mounts it. Risk is downtime, and whether the container held anything outside the volume.' },
    { q: 'You change `name = "beacon-api"` to `name = "beacon-api-v2"` with `create_before_destroy`. What happens at apply?', a: 'It works: the names differ, so both can coexist briefly. With the same name or the same host port it would fail during the create.' },
    { q: 'The configuration is unchanged but a container is corrupted. Which command replaces only it, and what does the plan say?', a: '`terraform apply -replace=docker_container.api`; the plan shows `will be replaced, as requested` for that address alone.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m06-05', moduleId: 'M06', title: 'Modules, environments and the configuration-management contrast', mins: 15,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m06-05', 'obj-m06-01'], prereqs: ['les-m06-03', 'les-m06-04'], refs: ['T17'],
  objective: 'Extract a reusable Beacon module without replacing anything (using `moved` blocks), separate staging and production state with a documented promotion path, choose between workspaces and directories with a reason, and state what Terraform does and does not do compared with a configuration-management tool.',
  model: 'A **module** is a directory of configuration with inputs and outputs, called from a `module` block; the root configuration is itself a module. Extracting one changes every resource\'s **address** (`docker_container.api` becomes `module.beacon.docker_container.api`), and to Terraform a new address is a new object — unless a **moved** block says it is the same one. **Environments** are separate state files with the same module and different inputs; the choice between **workspaces** (one configuration, several states behind one backend) and **directories** (one directory per environment with its own backend key) is about how strongly you want them separated. Terraform owns the **existence and shape** of objects; a configuration-management tool like Ansible owns **what happens inside them**. Both are idempotent; only one keeps a state file.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'Extracting a module and keeping the objects', body: 'The Beacon configuration has grown to the API, worker, database, network and volume, all in `main.tf`. Staging needs a copy. Move the five resources into `modules/beacon/main.tf`, replace hard-coded values with `variable` blocks (`beacon_version`, `api_port`, `db_volume_name`), add `output "api_endpoint"`, and call it:\n\n```\nmodule "beacon" {\n  source         = "./modules/beacon"\n  beacon_version = var.beacon_version\n  api_port       = 8080\n}\n```\n\n`terraform plan` now proposes to destroy `docker_container.api` and create `module.beacon.docker_container.api` — five destroys, five creates, including the database. The addresses changed; the objects did not. Tell it so:\n\n```\nmoved {\n  from = docker_container.db\n  to   = module.beacon.docker_container.db\n}\n```\n\nOne block per resource, and the plan becomes `docker_container.db has moved to module.beacon.docker_container.db` with `No changes` to any object. The `moved` blocks stay in the code until every state that uses this configuration has applied them, then they are removed. `terraform state mv` does the same by hand, without a plan and without a record in code; use it only when the refactor cannot be expressed as a block.' },
    { h: 'Environments: workspaces versus directories', body: 'A **workspace** (`terraform workspace new staging`) gives the same configuration a separate state under the same backend — in S3 it lands at `env:/staging/<key>` — and `terraform.workspace` can select values. It is quick, and it is the right tool when environments are identical except for a few inputs and share one backend, one set of credentials and one team.\n\nA **directory per environment** (`envs/staging/main.tf`, `envs/prod/main.tf`), each calling the module with its own `terraform.tfvars` and its own backend block, costs some repetition and buys separation: different backend buckets or keys, different provider credentials or accounts, different review rules, and no way to apply production by forgetting to switch workspace. The failure mode of workspaces is exactly that: `terraform workspace show` is a command you must remember to run. For anything where staging and production should not share credentials, directories win; workspaces fit short-lived variants of one environment (a per-branch preview).\n\nEither way the state key names the environment, the lock is per state, and `terraform plan` in one environment cannot touch the other.' },
    { h: 'A promotion path you can write down', body: 'With the module in place, a change moves through environments as a change to inputs, not to the module: `beacon_version = "1.4.3"` in `envs/staging/terraform.tfvars`, plan, apply, verify the user path; then the same value in `envs/prod/terraform.tfvars`, plan — which should show exactly the change staging showed — apply, verify. The module itself is versioned (a Git tag, `source = "git::https://git.corp.lab/platform/beacon-module.git?ref=v2.3.0"`) so production can pin a module version staging has already exercised.\n\nThe written path is short: what changes (the tfvars line), who reviews the plan, what evidence staging must produce before prod (a green user-path check, a no-change plan after apply), and how to roll back (set the previous version, plan, apply — a plan you have already seen in reverse). A promotion path that cannot be written in six lines is one that will be improvised at the worst moment.' },
    { h: 'What Terraform is not: the configuration-management contrast', body: 'Ansible, Chef, Puppet and the like run **inside** machines: install a package, write a file, start a service. They are idempotent — running a play twice converges to the same state — but they carry no state file; each run inspects the host and acts. They do not know whether the host should exist, and they will happily configure a server Terraform planned to destroy. Terraform, in turn, does not know what happened inside the container or instance after it was created; a package installed by hand is invisible to it, and it will never "drift-detect" the contents of a filesystem.\n\nThe boundary for Beacon: Terraform creates the network, the volume, the containers and (in AWS) the VPC, roles, ALB and RDS instance; the image build and whatever runs inside the container are owned by the Dockerfile (M07) and the process itself; a configuration-management tool would own an EC2 host\'s OS packages and agents if Beacon ran on EC2. Using Terraform `provisioner` blocks to run shell inside instances blurs the line and loses idempotency; using Ansible to create cloud resources loses the plan. Each tool, its layer, and a stated hand-off between them.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'The refactor that would have replaced the database', body: 'The team extracted `modules/beacon` and ran `terraform plan` in staging:\n\n```\nPlan: 5 to add, 0 to change, 5 to destroy.\n  # docker_container.db will be destroyed\n  # module.beacon.docker_container.db will be created\n  # docker_volume.pgdata will be destroyed\n  # module.beacon.docker_volume.pgdata will be created\n  ...\n```\n\nFive destroys including the volume with the staging data. They added five `moved` blocks, one per resource:\n\n```\nmoved { from = docker_volume.pgdata    to = module.beacon.docker_volume.pgdata }\nmoved { from = docker_container.db     to = module.beacon.docker_container.db }\nmoved { from = docker_container.api    to = module.beacon.docker_container.api }\nmoved { from = docker_container.worker to = module.beacon.docker_container.worker }\nmoved { from = docker_network.lab      to = module.beacon.docker_network.lab }\n```\n\nNext plan: `docker_volume.pgdata has moved to module.beacon.docker_volume.pgdata` (five such lines) and `No changes. Your infrastructure matches the configuration.` Apply recorded the new addresses; `terraform state list` showed only `module.beacon.*`. Production, in its own directory with its own backend key, received the same module and the same `moved` blocks a day later, after staging\'s user-path check was green; its plan showed the same five moves and nothing else. Then the `moved` blocks were deleted in a follow-up change, because both states had applied them.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'Moving a resource into a module is a refactor with no runtime effect.', right: 'Without `moved` blocks Terraform sees new addresses and plans destroy-and-create for every one, including stateful resources.' },
    { wrong: 'Workspaces are how you separate production from staging.', right: 'Workspaces share one backend and one set of credentials and rely on remembering `workspace select`. Directories give separate backends, credentials and review; workspaces fit short-lived variants.' },
    { wrong: 'Ansible and Terraform do the same job, so pick one.', right: 'Terraform owns whether objects exist and their shape, with a state file. Configuration management owns what happens inside a host, with no state. Each at its layer, with a stated hand-off.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict the plan after moving `docker_container.api` into `module.beacon` with no `moved` block.', a: '`docker_container.api will be destroyed` and `module.beacon.docker_container.api will be created`: 1 to add, 1 to destroy.' },
    { q: 'Staging and production must use different AWS accounts and credentials. Workspaces or directories, and why?', a: 'Directories: each has its own backend and provider configuration; workspaces share one backend and depend on remembering which one is selected.' },
    { q: 'A package is installed by hand inside an EC2 host Terraform created. What does the next `terraform plan` show?', a: 'Nothing: Terraform does not inspect inside the instance. That layer belongs to configuration management or the image.' }
  ]
});

/* ═══ M07 · Containers and image delivery ═══════════════════════════ */
LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m07-01', moduleId: 'M07', title: 'Images, layers and the process model', mins: 14,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m07-01'], prereqs: ['les-m02-01', 'les-m01-03'], refs: ['T16', 'T06'],
  objective: 'Explain what an image, a layer and a container are in terms of filesystems and processes, predict what `docker exec`, `docker diff` and `docker inspect` will show for a running Beacon container, and say why a container is not a small virtual machine.',
  model: 'An **image** is a stack of read-only **layers** — each a set of file changes — plus a small JSON configuration (the command to run, environment, working directory, exposed ports). A **container** is one process tree started from that image with a thin **writable layer** on top (the union filesystem shows all layers as one tree), inside a set of kernel **namespaces** (its own PID numbers, network interfaces, mounts, hostname) and under **cgroups** (CPU and memory limits). There is no guest kernel, no init system, no boot. When the process the container was started with exits, the container exits. Everything that is true of a Linux process in M02 is true of a container\'s process; the container just has a different view of the machine.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'Images are layers; layers are diffs', body: '`docker history registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.2` prints one line per layer, newest first: the `COPY . /app` layer of a few hundred kilobytes, the `pip install` layer of tens of megabytes, the base image\'s layers. Each layer is the set of files that instruction added, changed or deleted relative to the layer below; a layer is identified by a digest of its content, so two images built from the same base share those layers on disk and in the registry. Pulling an image that differs by one top layer transfers one layer.\n\nThe union filesystem (overlay2 on most hosts) presents the stack as one directory tree. A file deleted in an upper layer is hidden by a "whiteout" marker, not removed from the lower layer — which is why a secret `COPY`-ed in and `rm`-ed in a later `RUN` is still in the image. `docker image inspect` shows the configuration half: `Config.Cmd`, `Config.Entrypoint`, `Config.Env`, `Config.User`, `Config.ExposedPorts`, and `RootFS.Layers` with the digests.' },
    { h: 'A container is a process with a different view', body: '`docker run -d --name beacon-api registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.2` creates a writable layer over the image, sets up namespaces and cgroups, and starts the image\'s entrypoint as **PID 1 inside the container**. From the host, `ps -ef | grep beacon` shows the same process with an ordinary host PID; from inside, `docker exec beacon-api ps -ef` shows it as PID 1 and nothing else — no `systemd`, no `sshd`, no cron. The PID namespace renumbers; the network namespace gives the container its own `eth0` and loopback; the mount namespace gives it the union filesystem as `/`.\n\n`docker diff beacon-api` lists every file the running process has changed in its writable layer (`A /tmp/beacon.sock`, `C /app/cache`). That layer is deleted with `docker rm`; anything the process wrote that is not on a volume is gone with it. `docker inspect beacon-api` returns the live view: `State.Pid` (the host PID), `State.Status`, `NetworkSettings.IPAddress`, `HostConfig.Memory`, `Mounts`. It is the container equivalent of `systemctl show` plus `/proc/<pid>`.' },
    { h: 'Entrypoint, command and the exit of PID 1', body: 'The image configuration holds two pieces of the command line: `Entrypoint` (the executable, usually fixed) and `Cmd` (default arguments, overridden by whatever follows the image name in `docker run`). `ENTRYPOINT ["python", "-m", "beacon.api"]` with `CMD ["--port", "8080"]` runs `python -m beacon.api --port 8080`; `docker run ... registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.2 --port 9090` keeps the entrypoint and replaces the arguments. `docker run --entrypoint sh -it ...` replaces the executable for a debugging shell.\n\nThe container\'s lifetime is PID 1\'s lifetime. If the entrypoint is a shell script that starts Beacon in the background and exits, the container exits immediately with the script\'s code and `docker ps -a` shows `Exited (0) 2 seconds ago` while the logs look fine. If Beacon crashes, the container exits with Beacon\'s exit code: `Exited (1)` for an unhandled exception, `Exited (137)` when the kernel killed it (128 + 9, usually the memory cgroup limit — `docker inspect` shows `OOMKilled: true`), `Exited (143)` after a clean SIGTERM (128 + 15). M02\'s exit-code table applies unchanged.' },
    { h: 'Why "not a small VM" matters operationally', body: 'Because there is no init system, nothing restarts a crashed Beacon inside the container; the container exits and the **runtime** (Docker\'s restart policy, Compose, Kubernetes) decides whether to start a new one. Because there is one process tree, "log in and look around" is `docker exec`, and the tools you expect (`ss`, `curl`, `ps`) are only there if the image includes them — a slim image may have none, and the investigation moves to the host side with `nsenter -t <host pid> -n ss -ltnp`. Because the filesystem is layers, the image is immutable and the same bytes run everywhere; "it works on my machine" becomes "it works in this image", which is testable.\n\nAnd because the kernel is shared, a container is not a security boundary in the way a VM is: a process running as root in the container is root on the shared kernel unless a user namespace remaps it, which is why lesson 04 insists on `USER`.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'The container that exits immediately', body: 'After `docker run -d --name beacon-api registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.2`, `docker ps` is empty. Observations:\n\n```\n$ docker ps -a --filter name=beacon-api --format "{{.Status}}"\nExited (0) 3 seconds ago\n$ docker logs beacon-api\nstarting beacon api on :8080\n$ docker image inspect --format "{{.Config.Entrypoint}} {{.Config.Cmd}}" registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.2\n[/app/start.sh] []\n$ docker run --rm --entrypoint cat registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.2 /app/start.sh\n#!/bin/sh\npython -m beacon.api --port 8080 &\n```\n\n- Hypotheses: H1 Beacon crashed (would be a non-zero code and a traceback — ruled out by `Exited (0)` and the log); H2 PID 1 exited while Beacon ran as a background child; H3 a healthcheck killed it (Docker does not kill on unhealthy — ruled out).\n- Discriminating check: the entrypoint script backgrounds Beacon with `&` and reaches end of file; the shell, PID 1, exits 0; the kernel tears down the namespace and Beacon with it.\n- Smallest change: `exec python -m beacon.api --port 8080` so Beacon replaces the shell and becomes PID 1 — or drop the script and use `ENTRYPOINT ["python", "-m", "beacon.api"]`. Rebuild, run, `docker exec beacon-api ps -ef` shows `python` as PID 1, `curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8080/healthz` from the host (with the port published) returns `ok`.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'A container boots an operating system.', right: 'It starts one process in namespaces on the host kernel. There is no init, no boot, no guest kernel; when PID 1 exits the container is finished.' },
    { wrong: 'Deleting a file in a later Dockerfile instruction removes it from the image.', right: 'Layers are diffs; the file is hidden by a whiteout in the upper layer and still present in the lower one. Never copy a secret into a layer.' },
    { wrong: 'Files Beacon writes inside the container are kept.', right: 'They live in the writable layer, which `docker rm` deletes. Persistent data goes on a volume.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict what `docker exec beacon-api ps -ef` shows if the image\'s entrypoint is `["python", "-m", "beacon.api"]`.', a: 'One process, PID 1, `python -m beacon.api` (plus the `ps` itself). No init, no other services.' },
    { q: 'A container shows `Exited (137)`. What happened and which `docker inspect` field confirms it?', a: 'The kernel sent SIGKILL (128+9), typically the memory cgroup limit; `State.OOMKilled` is true.' },
    { q: 'Two images share the same base image. What does pulling the second one transfer?', a: 'Only the layers the first did not already bring; layers are content-addressed and shared on disk and in the registry.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m07-02', moduleId: 'M07', title: 'Dockerfile and the build context', mins: 15,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m07-01'], prereqs: ['les-m07-01', 'les-m04-02'], refs: ['T16', 'T06'],
  objective: 'Write a Dockerfile for Beacon with a pinned base image and pinned Python dependencies, order instructions so the cache survives code changes, keep secrets and junk out of the build context with .dockerignore, and explain what each instruction contributes to the image.',
  model: 'A Dockerfile is a recipe where each instruction either adds a **layer** (`FROM`, `RUN`, `COPY`, `ADD`) or edits the image **configuration** (`ENV`, `WORKDIR`, `USER`, `EXPOSE`, `ENTRYPOINT`, `CMD`, `HEALTHCHECK`). The build sends the **build context** — the directory you name, minus `.dockerignore` — to the builder, and `COPY` can only see that. The builder **caches** each layer by its instruction and its inputs; the first instruction whose inputs changed invalidates every layer after it. Reproducibility comes from pinning: the base image tag (or digest), and the Python packages in a lock or requirements file with exact versions.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'A Beacon Dockerfile, instruction by instruction', body: '```\nFROM python:3.12.7-slim-bookworm\n\nENV PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1\nWORKDIR /app\n\nCOPY requirements.txt .\nRUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\n\nCOPY --chown=beacon:beacon . .\n\nRUN useradd --system --uid 10001 --no-create-home beacon\nUSER beacon\n\nEXPOSE 8080\nHEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=3s --start-period=10s --retries=3 \\\n  CMD python -c "import urllib.request,sys; sys.exit(0 if urllib.request.urlopen(\'http://127.0.0.1:8080/healthz\', timeout=2).status == 200 else 1)"\nENTRYPOINT ["python", "-m", "beacon.api"]\nCMD ["--port", "8080"]\n```\n\n`FROM` pins an exact base tag; a floating `3.12` tag would change under you. `ENV` sets variables for build and runtime (unbuffered stdout so logs appear immediately). `WORKDIR` creates and selects the directory for later instructions. `COPY requirements.txt` then `RUN pip install` installs dependencies — `requirements.txt` carries exact versions (`fastapi==0.115.2`), so the install is the same tomorrow; `--no-cache-dir` keeps pip\'s download cache out of the layer. `COPY . .` brings the code. `EXPOSE` documents the port and does nothing else — it does not publish. `USER` switches the runtime user (lesson 04). `ENTRYPOINT` in exec form (a JSON array) runs Python directly as PID 1.' },
    { h: 'Build context and .dockerignore', body: '`docker build -t registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.2 .` — the trailing `.` is the context. The CLI packs that directory and sends it to the builder before the first instruction runs, so a context containing `.git`, a virtualenv, test fixtures and last week\'s database dump makes every build slow and, worse, makes `COPY . .` put all of it in the image. `.dockerignore` at the context root fixes both:\n\n```\n.git\n.venv\n__pycache__\n*.pyc\ntests/fixtures/*.dump\n.env\n*.pem\n```\n\n`.env` and `*.pem` are the lines that matter most: a credential file in the context is one careless `COPY` away from being a layer in a registry. The first line of `docker build` output, `transferring context: 1.2MB`, is the check — if it says 400MB, look at what you are sending. The context is also the boundary: `COPY ../shared/config.yaml .` fails because the builder cannot see above the context root; restructure or pass a second context.' },
    { h: 'The cache: order instructions by how often they change', body: 'The builder reuses a layer when the instruction is identical and its inputs (the files `COPY` would copy, the command text for `RUN`) are unchanged. Once one layer misses, every following layer is rebuilt. So the order is: things that never change (base image), things that change rarely (dependencies), things that change on every commit (code). The Beacon Dockerfile copies `requirements.txt` alone and installs before copying the rest, so a code edit rebuilds only the `COPY . .` layer and the few after it, in seconds, while a dependency bump rebuilds the install layer once.\n\n`docker build` output tells you: `CACHED [3/7] RUN pip install ...` versus a full pip run. Two things defeat the cache silently: `COPY . .` placed before the install (any file change reinstalls everything), and a `RUN apt-get update` on its own line (its text never changes, so it is cached forever and you get stale package lists — combine it with the install in one `RUN`). `--no-cache` forces a full rebuild when you suspect a stale layer.' },
    { h: 'Reproducible means pinned at every level', body: 'An image is reproducible if the same Dockerfile and context produce the same behaviour next month. That requires the base image pinned to a tag that does not move (`python:3.12.7-slim-bookworm`), or to a digest (`python@sha256:...`) for a guarantee; dependencies pinned to exact versions, ideally with hashes (`pip install --require-hashes`); and nothing in the build that downloads "the latest" of anything. Then tag the result with the application version (`1.4.2`), never with a floating tag that is overwritten by the next build, so a running container\'s tag tells you which code it carries.\n\n`docker image inspect --format "{{.Id}}"` gives the image id; the registry gives a digest on push. Lesson 05 covers why the digest, not the tag, is what you deploy by when it matters.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'A build that takes four minutes on every commit', body: 'Dockerfile before:\n\n```\nFROM python:3.12\nCOPY . /app\nWORKDIR /app\nRUN pip install -r requirements.txt\nCMD python app.py\n```\n\nSymptoms: `transferring context: 380MB`, every commit reruns `pip install` (`[3/4] RUN pip install` never shows `CACHED`), the image is 1.1GB, and `docker run` shows the process as `/bin/sh -c python app.py` with `sh` as PID 1.\n\nChanges, each with its reason: `.dockerignore` with `.git`, `.venv` and `data/` — context drops to 2MB. `COPY requirements.txt` before the install, code after — the install layer is `CACHED` on code-only commits; build time falls to twelve seconds. `FROM python:3.12.7-slim-bookworm` — a pinned, smaller base; image drops to 180MB. `requirements.txt` regenerated with exact versions. Exec-form `ENTRYPOINT ["python", "-m", "beacon.api"]` — Python is PID 1 and receives signals.\n\nVerification: `docker build` twice in a row reports every layer `CACHED` the second time; `docker history` shows the code layer at 1MB on top of a stable install layer; `docker run --rm registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.2 --help` prints Beacon\'s usage, proving the entrypoint and arguments compose.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: '`EXPOSE 8080` makes the port reachable from the host.', right: 'It only documents the port in the image configuration. Reachability needs `-p` (publish) at run time and a process listening on a non-loopback address.' },
    { wrong: 'The builder can copy any file on my machine.', right: 'Only files inside the build context, minus `.dockerignore`. That boundary is also what keeps credentials and the `.git` directory out of the image.' },
    { wrong: 'Putting `COPY . .` first is fine because Docker caches layers.', right: 'Any change to any file invalidates that layer and everything after it, including the dependency install. Copy dependency manifests first.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict which layers are rebuilt when only `beacon/api.py` changes, given `COPY requirements.txt` → `RUN pip install` → `COPY . .` → `USER beacon`.', a: 'From `COPY . .` onward (the copy and `USER`); the install layer is `CACHED`.' },
    { q: 'Why is `RUN apt-get update` on its own line a bug?', a: 'Its text never changes, so the layer is cached forever and later installs use stale package lists. Combine update and install in one `RUN`.' },
    { q: 'A file named `.env` with a database password sits in the project root. What two things prevent it from reaching the registry?', a: 'A `.dockerignore` entry for `.env`, and never relying on a later `rm` (layers keep deleted files).' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m07-03', moduleId: 'M07', title: 'Networks, ports, DNS and volumes', mins: 16,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m07-02', 'obj-m07-03'], prereqs: ['les-m07-01', 'les-m03-02', 'les-m02-05'], refs: ['T16'],
  objective: 'Trace a connection from a host port through Docker\'s publishing to the socket inside a container, repair a Beacon that listens on loopback, explain how containers find each other by name on a user-defined network, and choose a named volume or a bind mount for data that must outlive the container.',
  model: 'Each container has its own **network namespace**: its own `lo`, its own `eth0` with a private address on a Docker **bridge**. A **user-defined bridge network** adds an embedded **DNS server** at `127.0.0.11` inside each container that resolves other containers\' names on that network — the default bridge does not. **Publishing** a port (`-p 8080:8080`) makes the host listen on `0.0.0.0:8080` and forward to the container\'s `eth0` address — so a process inside listening on `127.0.0.1` is unreachable from the publish path, because the forwarded connection arrives on `eth0`, not on the container\'s loopback. A **named volume** is storage Docker manages that outlives any container; a **bind mount** is a host directory shown inside. Everything M03 taught about addresses, listeners and direction holds; the namespace just gives each container its own copy.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'Networks and the embedded DNS', body: '`docker network create beacon-net` makes a user-defined bridge. Containers started with `--network beacon-net` get an address on it and can reach each other by **container name** (or `--network-alias`): inside `beacon-api`, `getent hosts beacon-db` returns `172.20.0.3`, answered by the embedded resolver at `127.0.0.11` (`cat /etc/resolv.conf` inside the container shows it). Names resolve only within that network; a container on the default `bridge` cannot resolve `beacon-db`, and `docker run` without `--network` lands there — the classic reason "it cannot find the database" shows as `could not translate host name "beacon-db" to address`.\n\n`docker network inspect beacon-net` lists members and addresses; `docker inspect -f "{{.NetworkSettings.Networks}}" beacon-api` shows which networks a container joined. Containers on different user-defined networks cannot talk unless one joins both (`docker network connect`). That is the isolation primitive: a network per application, with the database reachable only from the network its clients are on.' },
    { h: 'Published ports and the loopback trap', body: '`-p 8080:8080` means host port 8080 (on all host addresses, `0.0.0.0`) forwards to container port 8080. `-p 127.0.0.1:8080:8080` publishes only on the host\'s loopback — the right choice for a database you only reach from the same machine. `docker port beacon-api` prints the mapping. Inside the container, Beacon must listen on an address the forwarded connection can reach: `0.0.0.0` (all interfaces in the container\'s namespace) or the `eth0` address. A server that binds `127.0.0.1:8080` — the default of many development servers — accepts connections only from inside the same namespace. The publish path arrives from outside and is refused; on the host you see:\n\n```\n$ curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8080/healthz\ncurl: (56) Recv failure: Connection reset by peer\n```\n\n(or `(52) Empty reply`, depending on the proxy path). The discriminating check is inside the container: `docker exec beacon-api ss -ltnp` showing `127.0.0.1:8080` instead of `0.0.0.0:8080` or `*:8080`. If the image lacks `ss`, use the host: `nsenter -t $(docker inspect -f "{{.State.Pid}}" beacon-api) -n ss -ltnp`. The fix is in Beacon\'s configuration: `--host 0.0.0.0` or `BEACON_LISTEN=0.0.0.0:8080`. Binding `0.0.0.0` inside the container is not an exposure — only published ports reach the host, and the container\'s network is private.' },
    { h: 'Named volumes versus bind mounts', body: 'A **named volume** (`docker volume create beacon-pgdata`; `-v beacon-pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data`) is a directory Docker manages under `/var/lib/docker/volumes/`, owned by the engine, listed by `docker volume ls`, and untouched by `docker rm` of the containers using it. Recreate the PostgreSQL container with a new image and the same volume, and the data is there. A **bind mount** (`-v /srv/beacon/config:/app/config:ro`) shows a host path inside the container: right for configuration you edit on the host, for source code during development, for a backup target; wrong for a database directory, because host permissions, ownership and filesystem semantics leak in and `docker volume` cannot manage it.\n\nTwo traps. Images like `postgres` declare `VOLUME /var/lib/postgresql/data`; starting without `-v` creates an **anonymous** volume with a random name that is easy to orphan or delete (`docker rm -v`). Always name it. And `docker compose down -v` deletes the named volumes the project created — the `-v` is the data-loss flag, and the lab asks you to prove persistence across `down` without it and recreation of the container with it.' },
    { h: 'Tracing a connection end to end', body: 'Host client → host `0.0.0.0:8080` listener (Docker) → forwarded to container `eth0` address port 8080 → Beacon\'s socket inside the namespace. Beacon → embedded DNS resolves `beacon-db` → container address on `beacon-net` → PostgreSQL socket on `0.0.0.0:5432` inside the db container (not published to the host at all). Each arrow has a check: `docker port`, `docker exec ... ss -ltnp`, `docker exec ... getent hosts beacon-db`, `docker network inspect`. The symptom tells you which arrow: a reset or empty reply from the host is the loopback bind; a name resolution error is the network membership; `Connection refused` from Beacon to `beacon-db` is PostgreSQL not listening yet (lesson 05\'s `depends_on` condition) or on the wrong port; a timeout between containers on the same bridge is almost always a host firewall rule.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'Reachable from inside, reset from outside', body: 'After `docker run -d --name beacon-api --network beacon-net -p 8080:8080 registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.2`:\n\n```\n$ curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8080/healthz\ncurl: (56) Recv failure: Connection reset by peer\n$ docker logs beacon-api | tail -1\nlistening on http://127.0.0.1:8080\n$ docker port beacon-api\n8080/tcp -> 0.0.0.0:8080\n$ docker exec beacon-api ss -ltn\nState  Recv-Q Send-Q Local Address:Port Peer Address:Port\nLISTEN 0      2048       127.0.0.1:8080      0.0.0.0:*\n$ docker exec beacon-api python -c "import urllib.request;print(urllib.request.urlopen(\'http://127.0.0.1:8080/healthz\').read())"\nb\'ok\'\n```\n\n- Hypotheses: H1 Beacon is not running (ruled out: the in-container request works); H2 the port is not published (ruled out: `docker port`); H3 Beacon listens on the container\'s loopback only, so the forwarded connection to `eth0` is refused and the host sees a reset.\n- H3 survives: `ss` shows `127.0.0.1:8080`. Smallest change: start with `-e BEACON_LISTEN=0.0.0.0:8080` (or the equivalent flag). Re-run; `ss` shows `0.0.0.0:8080`; the host `curl` returns `ok`.\n- Not the fix: `--network host`, which removes the namespace entirely and makes the symptom vanish while losing isolation and name resolution.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'Listening on `0.0.0.0` inside a container exposes it to the world.', right: 'It exposes it to the container\'s own network namespace. Only published ports reach the host, and only on the host address you choose.' },
    { wrong: 'Containers can always reach each other by name.', right: 'Only on a user-defined network, via the embedded DNS at `127.0.0.11`. The default bridge has no name resolution between containers.' },
    { wrong: 'A bind mount and a named volume are interchangeable for database data.', right: 'A named volume is engine-managed, survives `docker rm`, and avoids host permission and filesystem surprises. Bind mounts are for host files you edit, not for a data directory.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict the host-side symptom when Beacon inside the container binds `127.0.0.1:8080` and the port is published with `-p 8080:8080`.', a: 'The host `curl` gets a connection reset or empty reply; `docker exec ... ss -ltn` shows `127.0.0.1:8080`. In-container requests work.' },
    { q: 'Beacon logs `could not translate host name "beacon-db"`. Which `docker` command discriminates the cause?', a: '`docker inspect -f "{{.NetworkSettings.Networks}}"` on both containers: the api is on the default bridge or a different network from `beacon-db`.' },
    { q: 'Which command deletes the data in a Compose project\'s named volume, and which does not?', a: '`docker compose down -v` deletes the project\'s volumes; plain `docker compose down` and `docker rm` of a container leave named volumes intact.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m07-04', moduleId: 'M07', title: 'Non-root users, signals and health checks', mins: 15,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m07-01', 'obj-m07-04'], prereqs: ['les-m07-02', 'les-m02-01', 'les-m02-02'], refs: ['T16'],
  objective: 'Run Beacon as an unprivileged user with the file ownership that requires, make the process stop cleanly on SIGTERM as PID 1, and write a HEALTHCHECK whose status means something, explaining what Docker does and does not do with it.',
  model: 'Three facts of process life change inside a container. **User**: the process runs as whatever `USER` the image set, root by default, and root in a container is root on the shared kernel unless remapped — so the image declares an unprivileged user and owns its files accordingly. **Signals**: `docker stop` sends **SIGTERM** to PID 1, waits ten seconds, then **SIGKILL**; and the kernel gives PID 1 special treatment — a signal with no handler installed is ignored, not acted on — so a process that would die to SIGTERM anywhere else sits there as PID 1 until it is killed. **Health**: a `HEALTHCHECK` is a command Docker runs inside the container on a schedule and records as `starting`, `healthy` or `unhealthy`; Docker itself never restarts on unhealthy — orchestrators and Compose read the status and act.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'Running as a user that is not root', body: 'Create the user in the image and switch before the entrypoint:\n\n```\nRUN useradd --system --uid 10001 --no-create-home beacon\nCOPY --chown=beacon:beacon . /app\nUSER beacon\n```\n\n`--uid 10001` fixes the id so volumes and bind mounts can be owned consistently; `COPY --chown` makes the code readable without a `chmod -R` layer that doubles the image size. What the user loses: binding ports below 1024 (so Beacon listens on 8080, and the publish maps 80 or 443 if needed), writing anywhere it does not own (so `/app` is read-only in practice and writable paths — a cache, a socket — are a declared volume or `/tmp`), and installing packages at runtime (good). `docker exec beacon-api id` shows `uid=10001(beacon)`; `docker inspect -f "{{.Config.User}}"` shows what the image declared. A named volume mounted into the container is created owned by root unless the image\'s `VOLUME` path already has ownership baked in, so PostgreSQL images handle their own user and Beacon should write only under a path it owns.' },
    { h: 'PID 1 and SIGTERM', body: 'On a plain host, a Python process with no SIGTERM handler is terminated by the default action. As PID 1 in a container, the kernel does not apply default actions: a signal without a handler is dropped. So `docker stop beacon-api` sends SIGTERM, nothing happens, ten seconds pass, SIGKILL ends it: exit code 137, in-flight requests dropped, PostgreSQL connections never closed. `time docker stop beacon-api` showing `10.1s` is the tell.\n\nTwo fixes, and use both. In the application, install a handler that stops accepting, finishes in-flight work and exits 0:\n\n```\nimport signal, sys\ndef _term(signum, frame):\n    server.shutdown()        # stop accepting, drain\n    db.close()\n    sys.exit(0)\nsignal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, _term)\n```\n\nIn the image, make sure Python **is** PID 1: exec-form `ENTRYPOINT ["python", "-m", "beacon.api"]`, not the shell form `CMD python -m beacon.api`, which makes `/bin/sh -c` PID 1 — the shell gets the SIGTERM and does not forward it to its child. If a shell script must wrap the start, end it with `exec`. For images whose process cannot be changed, `docker run --init` (Compose: `init: true`) inserts a tiny init as PID 1 that forwards signals and reaps zombies. `STOPSIGNAL` changes which signal `docker stop` sends for processes that expect another (PostgreSQL prefers SIGINT for a fast shutdown). `docker stop -t 30` or Compose `stop_grace_period: 30s` lengthens the grace period when draining takes longer than ten seconds.' },
    { h: 'HEALTHCHECK: what it measures and who acts on it', body: '`HEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=3s --start-period=10s --retries=3 CMD <command>` runs the command inside the container every interval. Exit 0 is healthy, 1 is unhealthy; after `retries` consecutive failures the container\'s status flips to `unhealthy`; during `start-period` failures do not count toward retries. The command runs with the image\'s tools and user: `curl` only works if the image has it, which slim Python images do not, so the Beacon check uses `python -c` with `urllib`. `docker inspect -f "{{.State.Health.Status}}" beacon-api` shows the current status; `{{json .State.Health}}` shows the last few results with output, which is where the failing check\'s error text lives.\n\nDocker Engine does nothing with the status except display it. It does not restart an unhealthy container; `docker ps` shows `(unhealthy)` and that is all. Compose uses it for `depends_on: condition: service_healthy` (lesson 05) and Kubernetes ignores it entirely in favour of its own probes (M08). The check should test the thing clients need — an HTTP `200` from `/healthz` that confirms the process can serve a request — not a `true` that passes while the server is wedged, and not a deep check of the database that marks Beacon unhealthy when PostgreSQL is the one that is down.' },
    { h: 'Proving it', body: 'Three pieces of evidence close this objective. **User**: `docker exec beacon-api id -u` prints `10001`, and `docker exec beacon-api touch /app/x` fails with `Permission denied`. **Signals**: `docker stop beacon-api` returns in under a second, `docker inspect -f "{{.State.ExitCode}}"` is `0` (or `143` if the handler re-raises the default), and the log ends with a shutdown line rather than nothing. **Health**: after start, status moves `starting` → `healthy`; stop PostgreSQL and the API stays `healthy` (because the check is about the API) while a request that needs the database fails with a clear `503` from Beacon; break Beacon\'s listener and status moves to `unhealthy` within `interval × retries`. Each is a before/after pair, recorded.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'Ten seconds to stop', body: 'The lab\'s Compose stack took exactly ten seconds to stop the API and PostgreSQL logged `unexpected EOF on client connection` each time.\n\n```\n$ time docker stop beacon-api\nreal    0m10.12s\n$ docker inspect -f "{{.State.ExitCode}}" beacon-api\n137\n$ docker image inspect -f "{{.Config.Entrypoint}} {{.Config.Cmd}}" registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.2\n[] [/bin/sh -c python -m beacon.api]\n```\n\n- Exit 137 after a ten-second wait is the SIGKILL fallback. Two causes stack: the shell form makes `sh` PID 1, so Python never sees the SIGTERM; and Beacon had no handler anyway.\n- Change one: `ENTRYPOINT ["python", "-m", "beacon.api"]`. Re-test: still `10.1s` — Python is PID 1 now, but as PID 1 the unhandled SIGTERM is dropped.\n- Change two: the `signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, _term)` handler that drains and exits 0. Re-test: `real 0m0.41s`, exit code `0`, log line `shutdown complete, 2 requests drained`, no EOF errors in PostgreSQL.\n\nThe before/after pair with both exit codes is the evidence. `--init` would have fixed the forwarding but not the draining; the handler is what made the stop clean.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'Docker restarts a container when its health check fails.', right: 'Docker only records and displays the status. Compose and orchestrators act on it; a restart policy reacts to exit, not to health.' },
    { wrong: 'Python exits on SIGTERM by default, so no handler is needed.', right: 'Not as PID 1: the kernel drops signals PID 1 has no handler for. Install a handler, run in exec form, or add `--init`.' },
    { wrong: 'Running as root inside a container is harmless because the container is isolated.', right: 'The kernel is shared; root in the container is root on the host\'s kernel unless user namespaces remap it. Declare `USER` and own files accordingly.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict `time docker stop` and the exit code for a container whose `CMD` is the shell form `python -m beacon.api` with no signal handler.', a: 'About ten seconds and exit code 137: `sh` is PID 1 and does not forward SIGTERM, so Docker falls back to SIGKILL.' },
    { q: 'A Beacon container shows `(unhealthy)` in `docker ps`. Is it going to be restarted, and where is the check\'s error text?', a: 'Not by Docker Engine. `docker inspect -f "{{json .State.Health}}"` holds the last results and their output.' },
    { q: 'Why does the Beacon HEALTHCHECK use `python -c` with urllib rather than `curl`?', a: 'The check runs inside the container with the image\'s tools; a slim Python image has no curl, and a missing command makes every check fail.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m07-05', moduleId: 'M07', title: 'Architectures, digests and Compose', mins: 16,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m07-05', 'obj-m07-03'], prereqs: ['les-m07-03', 'les-m07-04'], refs: ['T06', 'T16'],
  objective: 'Inspect an image for its platform and digest, diagnose `exec format error` as an architecture mismatch, build a multi-platform image, deploy by digest when the tag could move, and compose Beacon\'s API, worker and PostgreSQL with a named volume and readiness-gated startup.',
  model: 'A **tag** (`1.4.2`) is a mutable pointer in a registry; a **digest** (`sha256:…`) is the content hash of a manifest and cannot change. A tag may point at a single-platform **manifest** or at a **manifest list** (an index) that maps platforms — `linux/amd64`, `linux/arm64` — to per-platform manifests; the client picks the one matching its CPU, and if none matches you get the wrong binary and the kernel says `exec format error`. **Compose** is a file that declares services, networks and volumes for one application and runs them together; `depends_on` with `condition: service_healthy` is how startup waits for a health check instead of hoping.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'Platforms, manifest lists and exec format error', body: 'On an ARM Mac, `docker build -t registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.2 .` produces a `linux/arm64` image. Push it, pull it on an `amd64` node, and:\n\n```\n$ docker run --rm registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.2\nexec /usr/local/bin/python: exec format error\n```\n\nThe kernel cannot execute an ARM binary. In Kubernetes the same line appears in `kubectl logs` with the Pod in `CrashLoopBackOff`, and the tempting wrong hypothesis is scheduling or probes. The discriminating check is the manifest:\n\n```\n$ docker buildx imagetools inspect registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.2\nName:      registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.2\nMediaType: application/vnd.oci.image.manifest.v1+json\nDigest:    sha256:7c1e...\nPlatform:  linux/arm64\n```\n\nA single manifest with `Platform: linux/arm64`, no list. Locally, `docker image inspect -f "{{.Os}}/{{.Architecture}}" registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.2` says the same. The fix is to build for the platforms you run: `docker buildx build --platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64 -t registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.2 --push .` produces an index with both, and `imagetools inspect` then lists `Manifests:` with one entry per platform. `--platform linux/amd64` alone builds for the target under emulation when you only need one. Pulling with `--platform` lets you fetch a specific variant to test.' },
    { h: 'Digest versus tag', body: 'After the push: `docker buildx imagetools inspect ... | grep Digest` or `docker image inspect -f "{{index .RepoDigests 0}}"` gives `registry.lab/beacon/api@sha256:9b3a…`. That string identifies exactly these bytes forever. The tag `1.4.2` identifies whatever the registry currently has under that name; a rebuild and re-push with the same tag changes it silently, and two nodes pulling an hour apart can run different code under the same tag. Deploying by digest (`image: registry.lab/beacon/api@sha256:9b3a…`) removes that possibility; M10 makes it the promotion rule. For the same reason a floating tag is never used in this course: it says nothing about which code is running, and a cache may serve an old one.\n\n`docker pull` reports `Digest: sha256:…` for what it fetched; comparing that with the digest recorded at build time is the proof that the node runs the artefact that was tested.' },
    { h: 'Compose: one file for the application', body: '```\nservices:\n  db:\n    image: postgres:16.6-bookworm\n    environment:\n      POSTGRES_USER: beacon\n      POSTGRES_PASSWORD_FILE: /run/secrets/db_password\n      POSTGRES_DB: beacon\n    volumes:\n      - pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data\n    secrets: [db_password]\n    healthcheck:\n      test: ["CMD-SHELL", "pg_isready -U beacon -d beacon"]\n      interval: 5s\n      timeout: 3s\n      retries: 10\n  api:\n    image: registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.2\n    environment:\n      BEACON_LISTEN: 0.0.0.0:8080\n      BEACON_DB_URL: postgres://beacon@db:5432/beacon\n    ports: ["127.0.0.1:8080:8080"]\n    depends_on:\n      db:\n        condition: service_healthy\n    stop_grace_period: 20s\n  worker:\n    image: registry.lab/beacon/worker:1.4.2\n    environment:\n      BEACON_DB_URL: postgres://beacon@db:5432/beacon\n    depends_on:\n      db:\n        condition: service_healthy\nvolumes:\n  pgdata:\nsecrets:\n  db_password:\n    file: ./secrets/db_password\n```\n\nCompose creates a network named after the project; services resolve each other by service name (`db`), which is why `BEACON_DB_URL` says `db` and nothing else. `pgdata` is a named volume; `docker compose down` keeps it, `down -v` deletes it. The database port is not published at all. A top-level `version:` key is obsolete and ignored by the current Compose; leave it out.' },
    { h: 'depends_on: order versus readiness', body: 'Plain `depends_on: [db]` only orders the **start** of containers: PostgreSQL\'s process begins before Beacon\'s, and Beacon connects a second later to a server still initialising its data directory, gets `Connection refused`, and crashes — the race that produces "works on the second `up`". `condition: service_healthy` makes Compose wait until the dependency\'s healthcheck reports healthy before starting the dependent, which is why `db` carries a `pg_isready` check. `condition: service_started` is the old behaviour spelled out; `service_completed_successfully` waits for a one-shot container (a migration job) to exit 0.\n\nThis is readiness at the Compose layer only. Beacon still needs its own retry on connect, because the database can go away after startup, and Kubernetes has no `depends_on` at all — there, readiness probes and retries do the job (M08). The lab asks for the proof in both directions: `docker compose up -d` shows `db` reaching `healthy` before `api` starts, and `docker compose down && docker compose up -d` brings back the same rows from `pgdata`.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'CrashLoopBackOff that was not a Kubernetes problem', body: 'Week 21. Beacon deployed to the kind cluster from the registry; Pods crash-looping. `kubectl logs beacon-api-6d9f-x2k7q` prints one line: `exec /usr/local/bin/python: exec format error`.\n\n- Hypotheses: H1 the node lacks resources (would be `Pending`, not crashing); H2 a probe kills it (would show `Liveness probe failed` events and a running process first); H3 the image is the wrong architecture for the node.\n- Checks: `kubectl get node -o jsonpath="{.items[0].status.nodeInfo.architecture}"` → `amd64`. `docker buildx imagetools inspect registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.2` → a single manifest, `Platform: linux/arm64`. H3.\n- Fix: `docker buildx build --platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64 -t registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.2 --push .`; `imagetools inspect` now lists both platforms under one index digest `sha256:51aa…`. Update the Deployment to `image: registry.lab/beacon/api@sha256:51aa…` so the node pulls exactly that index; `kubectl rollout status` reports success; `kubectl exec` into a Pod and `uname -m` prints `x86_64`; the user path through the Service returns `202`.\n\nThe cluster was never the problem; the manifest said so in one command.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: '`exec format error` means the image is corrupt or the entrypoint path is wrong.', right: 'It means the binary is for a different CPU architecture than the node. Inspect the manifest\'s platform and build for the platforms you run.' },
    { wrong: 'A tag like `1.4.2` always refers to the same image.', right: 'Tags are mutable pointers; a re-push moves them. The digest is the immutable identity, and deploying by digest is how you know what runs.' },
    { wrong: '`depends_on` guarantees the database is ready.', right: 'It orders container start. Only `condition: service_healthy` with a healthcheck on the dependency waits for readiness, and the client still needs its own retry.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict the first line of `kubectl logs` when an arm64-only image is scheduled on an amd64 node.', a: '`exec /usr/local/bin/python: exec format error` (the entrypoint binary cannot run); the Pod goes to CrashLoopBackOff.' },
    { q: 'Which command shows whether a tag points at a manifest list and which platforms it contains?', a: '`docker buildx imagetools inspect <image:tag>`; a list shows `Manifests:` with a `Platform:` per entry.' },
    { q: 'Compose: `api` has `depends_on: [db]` with no condition. What happens on the first `up` if PostgreSQL takes three seconds to initialise?', a: 'The api starts immediately, gets `Connection refused` from `db`, and crashes or restarts; add a healthcheck on `db` and `condition: service_healthy`.' }
  ]
});

/* ═══ M08 · Kubernetes fundamentals ═════════════════════════════════ */
LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m08-01', moduleId: 'M08', title: 'Control plane, nodes, controllers and desired state', mins: 14,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m08-01'], prereqs: ['les-m07-01', 'les-m07-05', 'les-m01-03'], refs: ['T04', 'T02'],
  objective: 'Name what the API server, etcd, scheduler, controller manager, kubelet and kube-proxy each do, explain reconciliation as the gap between an object\'s spec and its status, and read that gap from `kubectl describe` and events on a local kind cluster.',
  model: 'Kubernetes is a database of **desired state** plus a set of loops that make reality match it. You write an object (a Deployment with `replicas: 2`) to the **API server**, which validates it and stores it in **etcd**. **Controllers** watch for objects whose **status** does not match their **spec** and act: the Deployment controller creates a ReplicaSet, the ReplicaSet controller creates Pods, the **scheduler** assigns each unscheduled Pod to a node, and the **kubelet** on that node starts the containers and reports status back. **kube-proxy** programs the node so Service addresses reach Pods. Nothing runs your request directly; everything is a loop noticing a gap. Every symptom is therefore a gap somewhere, and the object\'s status and its **events** say which loop is stuck and why.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'The components and their one job each', body: 'The **API server** is the only thing anything talks to: `kubectl`, controllers and kubelets all read and write objects through it, and it authenticates, authorises and validates. **etcd** stores every object; losing it loses the cluster\'s memory, which is why it is the thing you back up. The **scheduler** watches for Pods with no `nodeName` and picks a node that satisfies their requests and constraints. The **controller manager** runs the built-in loops: Deployment, ReplicaSet, Node, Job, EndpointSlice and others. On each node the **kubelet** watches for Pods assigned to it, asks the container runtime to run them, runs probes, and writes `status` back. **kube-proxy** (or the network plugin replacing it) keeps each node\'s packet rules in step with Services and EndpointSlices. **CoreDNS** is an ordinary Deployment that answers for `*.svc.cluster.local`.\n\nIn kind, all of these run as containers inside one Docker container per "node": `kind create cluster --image kindest/node:v1.31.2` and `kubectl get pods -n kube-system` lists them. The versions are pinned in the lab profile; `kubectl version` prints both client and server, and a skew of more than one minor is the first thing to rule out when `kubectl` behaves oddly.' },
    { h: 'spec is what you want; status is what you have', body: 'Every object has `metadata` (name, namespace, labels), `spec` (desired) and `status` (observed, written by controllers — you do not edit it). `kubectl get deployment beacon-api -o yaml` shows `spec.replicas: 2` next to `status.readyReplicas: 1`: that difference is the whole story of an incident, and `kubectl get` columns like `READY 1/2` are shorthand for it. `kubectl apply -f` sends your desired state; the server stores it and returns immediately — a successful `apply` means the request was accepted, not that anything has happened yet. `kubectl rollout status` and `kubectl wait --for=condition=Available` are how you wait for the loops to close the gap.\n\nThe same reconciliation makes Kubernetes self-healing: delete a Pod and the ReplicaSet sees `status` below `spec` and creates another; drain a node and the Pods are rescheduled elsewhere. It also makes direct edits pointless: `kubectl edit pod` to change an image is reverted or ignored because the ReplicaSet owns the Pod\'s template. Change the object at the top of the ownership chain.' },
    { h: 'Events: the loops explaining themselves', body: 'When a controller or the kubelet cannot close a gap, it records an **Event** against the object: `FailedScheduling`, `Pulling`, `Failed` (image pull), `Unhealthy` (probe), `BackOff`, `FailedMount`. `kubectl describe pod <name>` ends with the recent events for that Pod; `kubectl get events -n beacon --sort-by=.lastTimestamp` lists the namespace\'s in time order; `kubectl describe deployment` shows the Deployment\'s own conditions (`Progressing`, `Available`) with reasons such as `ProgressDeadlineExceeded`. Events are kept for about an hour by default, so capture them early.\n\nReading order for any unhappy Pod: `kubectl get pod` for the phase and `READY` column, `kubectl describe pod` for conditions and events, then `kubectl logs` (add `--previous` for the last crashed container). The phase tells you which loop: `Pending` with no node is the scheduler; `ContainerCreating` is the kubelet pulling or mounting; `Running` but not ready is probes; `CrashLoopBackOff` is the process inside.' },
    { h: 'Namespaces, labels and the chain of ownership', body: 'A **namespace** scopes names: `beacon-api` in `beacon` and `beacon-api` in `staging` are different objects. `kubectl -n beacon` or `kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=beacon` chooses one; forgetting it is the most common reason for `No resources found`. **Labels** are the glue: a ReplicaSet selects its Pods by label, a Service selects its backends by label, and `kubectl get pods -l app=beacon-api` is how you find the Pods a Deployment created. **ownerReferences** in a Pod\'s metadata point at its ReplicaSet, and the ReplicaSet\'s at its Deployment; garbage collection follows them, so deleting a Deployment deletes its ReplicaSets and Pods.\n\n`kubectl get all -n beacon` shows the chain at a glance: one Deployment, its ReplicaSets (the current one with the desired count, old ones at zero), the Pods, and the Service. Reading this output until it is boring is the fastest way to internalise the model.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'A Pod that stays Pending', body: 'After `kubectl apply -f deploy/beacon-api.yaml`:\n\n```\n$ kubectl -n beacon get pods\nNAME                          READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE\nbeacon-api-7c9d8b5f4-k2x9p    1/1     Running   0          2m\nbeacon-api-7c9d8b5f4-r7qzt    0/1     Pending   0          2m\n$ kubectl -n beacon describe pod beacon-api-7c9d8b5f4-r7qzt | tail -4\nEvents:\n  Type     Reason            Age   From               Message\n  ----     ------            ----  ----               -------\n  Warning  FailedScheduling  2m    default-scheduler  0/1 nodes are available: 1 Insufficient memory. preemption: 0/1 nodes are available: 1 No preemption victims found for incoming pod.\n```\n\n- The gap: Deployment `spec.replicas: 2`, `status.availableReplicas: 1`. The second Pod has no `nodeName`; the loop that is stuck is the scheduler, and its event says why: the Pod requests more memory than the single kind node has free.\n- Hypotheses the event rules out: the image (never pulled yet), probes (never started), the Service (irrelevant to scheduling).\n- Discriminating check: `kubectl describe node | grep -A5 "Allocated resources"` shows memory requests at 92%; the Pod template asks for `2Gi`.\n- Smallest change: the request is a typo for `256Mi`; fix the Deployment\'s template (not the Pod), `kubectl apply`, and the rollout replaces both Pods. `kubectl get pods` shows `2/2 Running`; the Deployment condition `Available` is `True`.\n\nThe user-path verification follows in lesson 03; here the evidence is that the spec/status gap closed and the events say the scheduler is satisfied.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: '`kubectl apply` succeeded, so the change has happened.', right: 'Apply stores desired state and returns. Controllers close the gap afterwards; `rollout status`, `wait` and the object\'s status show whether they did.' },
    { wrong: 'Editing a Pod directly is the quickest fix.', right: 'The ReplicaSet owns the Pod\'s template and will recreate Pods from it. Change the Deployment; the loops propagate it.' },
    { wrong: 'The kubelet decides where Pods run.', right: 'The scheduler assigns a node by writing `nodeName`; the kubelet on that node then runs what was assigned to it and reports status.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict which component recorded the event and what it means: `Warning FailedScheduling ... 0/1 nodes are available: 1 Insufficient cpu`.', a: 'The scheduler; no node has enough unreserved CPU for the Pod\'s requests, so the Pod stays Pending with no node assigned.' },
    { q: 'A Deployment shows `READY 1/3`. Which two fields are being compared, and who writes each?', a: '`spec.replicas` (you) versus `status.readyReplicas` (the Deployment controller from Pod readiness reported by kubelets).' },
    { q: 'You delete one Pod of a two-replica Deployment. What happens and which loop does it?', a: 'The ReplicaSet controller sees one Pod fewer than `spec.replicas` and creates a replacement; the scheduler places it, the kubelet starts it.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m08-02', moduleId: 'M08', title: 'Pods, Deployments, ReplicaSets and rollouts', mins: 15,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m08-02'], prereqs: ['les-m08-01'], refs: ['T04'],
  objective: 'Write a Deployment for Beacon, explain how a Deployment manages ReplicaSets which manage Pods, demonstrate self-healing, perform a rolling update with chosen surge and unavailability, read the rollout history and undo a bad revision.',
  model: 'A **Pod** is the unit the kubelet runs: one or more containers sharing a network namespace and volumes, with an address that dies with it. Pods are not repaired; they are replaced. A **ReplicaSet** keeps N Pods matching a label selector alive from one Pod template. A **Deployment** owns ReplicaSets: every change to the Pod template creates a new ReplicaSet and shifts replicas from the old one to the new one at a pace set by `maxSurge` and `maxUnavailable` — the **rollout**. Old ReplicaSets are kept at zero replicas as **revisions**, which is what makes **undo** a one-line operation. You write the Deployment; you read the ReplicaSets and Pods to see what it did.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'The Deployment manifest, and what each part controls', body: '```\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: beacon-api\n  namespace: beacon\nspec:\n  replicas: 2\n  selector:\n    matchLabels: { app: beacon-api }\n  strategy:\n    type: RollingUpdate\n    rollingUpdate: { maxSurge: 1, maxUnavailable: 0 }\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels: { app: beacon-api }\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: api\n          image: registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.2\n          ports: [{ name: http, containerPort: 8080 }]\n          resources:\n            requests: { cpu: 100m, memory: 128Mi }\n            limits: { memory: 256Mi }\n```\n\n`selector.matchLabels` must match `template.metadata.labels` or the API rejects it; the selector is immutable after creation. `replicas` is the desired count. `strategy` is how a template change is rolled out. The `template` is a Pod spec: everything under it is copied into each Pod the ReplicaSet creates, so changing anything there (image, env, resources, probes) is a rollout; changing `replicas` alone is a scale, not a rollout. `apps/v1` is the only API group for Deployments.' },
    { h: 'Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod, and self-healing', body: 'After `kubectl apply`, `kubectl -n beacon get rs` shows `beacon-api-7c9d8b5f4` — the Deployment name plus a hash of the Pod template — with `DESIRED 2 CURRENT 2 READY 2`, and the Pods are `beacon-api-7c9d8b5f4-<random>`. The hash is also added as the label `pod-template-hash` so each ReplicaSet selects only its own Pods.\n\nSelf-healing is the ReplicaSet loop: `kubectl delete pod beacon-api-7c9d8b5f4-k2x9p` and within a second `kubectl get pods` shows a new name in `ContainerCreating`, then `Running`. The deleted Pod is gone for good, including its address and its writable layer; anything it had not written elsewhere is lost, which is the same statelessness requirement as M05 and M07. Scaling is `kubectl scale deployment beacon-api --replicas=3` or a change to `spec.replicas`; the ReplicaSet adds a Pod with no new revision.' },
    { h: 'Rollouts: surge, unavailability and the progress deadline', body: 'Change the image: `kubectl -n beacon set image deployment/beacon-api api=registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.3` (or edit the manifest and `apply`; the manifest is the reviewable form). The Deployment creates a second ReplicaSet with the new hash and moves replicas across. With `maxSurge: 1, maxUnavailable: 0` and two replicas, it may run up to three Pods at once and must keep two ready: new Pod up and ready, then one old Pod down, repeat. With the defaults (`25%` each, rounded), a two-replica Deployment surges one and allows one unavailable, so there can be a moment with one old ready Pod serving. `maxUnavailable: 0` is the choice for a service that must not lose capacity; `maxSurge: 0` is the choice when the cluster cannot fit an extra Pod.\n\nReadiness (lesson 05) is what "ready" means here: a new Pod that never passes its readiness probe never counts, so the rollout stops at the first bad Pod instead of replacing everything with something broken. After `progressDeadlineSeconds` (default 600) with no progress, the condition `Progressing` becomes `False` with reason `ProgressDeadlineExceeded`; the Deployment does not roll back on its own. `kubectl rollout status deployment/beacon-api` follows the rollout and exits non-zero on that condition, which is what a pipeline gate checks.' },
    { h: 'History and undo', body: '`kubectl rollout history deployment/beacon-api` lists revisions; the `CHANGE-CAUSE` column is filled from the annotation `kubernetes.io/change-cause` on the Deployment (set it with `kubectl annotate deployment/beacon-api kubernetes.io/change-cause="api 1.4.3"` or in the manifest; the old `--record` flag is gone). `--revision=3` shows that revision\'s template. `kubectl rollout undo deployment/beacon-api` returns to the previous revision; `--to-revision=2` picks one. Undo is itself a rollout — it scales the old ReplicaSet (still present at zero) back up under the same surge rules — so it is as safe as the forward change and shows in history as a new revision with the old template. `revisionHistoryLimit` (default 10) bounds how many old ReplicaSets are kept.\n\nUndo restores the template, not the world: a database migration shipped with 1.4.3 is not undone by rolling the API back (M10 covers compatibility). And undo is only right when the previous revision was good; verify the user path after it as after any rollout.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'Rolling out a bad image and getting back', body: '```\n$ kubectl -n beacon set image deployment/beacon-api api=registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.3\n$ kubectl -n beacon rollout status deployment/beacon-api --timeout=120s\nWaiting for deployment "beacon-api" rollout to finish: 1 out of 2 new replicas have been updated...\nerror: timed out waiting for the condition\n$ kubectl -n beacon get rs\nNAME                    DESIRED   CURRENT   READY   AGE\nbeacon-api-7c9d8b5f4    2         2         2       40m\nbeacon-api-5f6b7d9c8    1         1         0       2m\n$ kubectl -n beacon get pods -l pod-template-hash=5f6b7d9c8\nNAME                          READY   STATUS             RESTARTS   AGE\nbeacon-api-5f6b7d9c8-m4wq2    0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   4          2m\n$ kubectl -n beacon logs beacon-api-5f6b7d9c8-m4wq2 --previous | tail -1\nKeyError: \'BEACON_QUEUE_URL\'\n```\n\n- The new ReplicaSet has one Pod, never ready; the old one still has both because `maxUnavailable: 0` forbade removing them. Users were served by the old Pods throughout. The readiness gate did its job.\n- Cause from the log: 1.4.3 requires a variable the Deployment does not set. The fix is a config change plus the image, reviewed together; for now, restore service state: `kubectl rollout undo deployment/beacon-api`.\n- `kubectl rollout status` completes; `get rs` shows `5f6b7d9c8` at `0` and `7c9d8b5f4` at `2`; `rollout history` shows revision 3 with the 1.4.2 template. The user path through the Service returns `202`. Then the real change: add `BEACON_QUEUE_URL` from a ConfigMap (lesson 04) and roll 1.4.3 again.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'A rollout replaces every Pod with the new version regardless of health.', right: 'New Pods must pass readiness before old ones are removed, within `maxSurge`/`maxUnavailable`. A broken image stalls the rollout with the old Pods still serving.' },
    { wrong: '`rollout undo` reverts everything the release changed.', right: 'It restores the previous Pod template by scaling the old ReplicaSet back up. ConfigMaps, Secrets, migrations and other objects are untouched.' },
    { wrong: 'Changing `replicas` creates a new revision.', right: 'Scaling changes the count on the current ReplicaSet. Only a change to the Pod template creates a new ReplicaSet and revision.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict: two replicas, `maxSurge: 1`, `maxUnavailable: 0`, new image crash-loops. How many old Pods are serving while the rollout is stuck?', a: 'Both. One new Pod exists but is not ready, and `maxUnavailable: 0` forbids removing an old Pod until a new one is ready.' },
    { q: 'What names the ReplicaSet a Deployment creates, and why does the name change on an image bump?', a: 'The Deployment name plus a hash of the Pod template; a template change produces a new hash and therefore a new ReplicaSet.' },
    { q: 'Which annotation fills `CHANGE-CAUSE` in `kubectl rollout history`?', a: '`kubernetes.io/change-cause` on the Deployment, set in the manifest or with `kubectl annotate`.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m08-03', moduleId: 'M08', title: 'Services and EndpointSlices', mins: 15,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m08-03'], prereqs: ['les-m08-02', 'les-m03-03'], refs: ['T02'],
  objective: 'Explain how a Service selects ready Pods into EndpointSlices and how `port` differs from `targetPort`, trace a request from a Service name to an actual HTTP response from a Pod, and repair a selector or targetPort mismatch from the evidence.',
  model: 'A **Service** is a stable name and virtual address in front of a changing set of Pods. Its **selector** picks Pods by label; the EndpointSlice controller writes the addresses of the matching Pods that are **Ready** into **EndpointSlices**; kube-proxy on every node turns "connect to the Service address" into "connect to one of those endpoints". The Service\'s `port` is what clients dial; `targetPort` is the container port (a number or a named port) the traffic is delivered to. DNS gives the Service a name, `beacon-api.beacon.svc.cluster.local`. A Service with no endpoints is not an error anywhere — it is a Service whose selector matched nothing ready, and the connection simply fails.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'The Service manifest and the two ports', body: '```\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: beacon-api\n  namespace: beacon\nspec:\n  type: ClusterIP\n  selector:\n    app: beacon-api\n  ports:\n    - name: http\n      port: 80\n      targetPort: http      # the container port named http (8080)\n      protocol: TCP\n```\n\nClients inside the cluster use `http://beacon-api.beacon.svc.cluster.local:80` (or `beacon-api` from the same namespace); the Service forwards to port 8080 on a Pod because the Pod\'s container declares `ports: [{name: http, containerPort: 8080}]`. A numeric `targetPort: 8080` works too and is the common mistake site: the Pod listens on 8080, the Service says `targetPort: 8000`, and every connection is refused by the Pod. A named port moves the number into one place — the Pod template — so the Service cannot drift from it. `type: ClusterIP` is reachable only inside the cluster; `NodePort` also opens a port on every node; `LoadBalancer` asks the cloud for an external address. For local testing, `kubectl port-forward svc/beacon-api 8080:80` connects your laptop to the Service without changing its type.' },
    { h: 'Selectors, readiness and EndpointSlices', body: 'The selector is a label query. The EndpointSlice controller watches Pods and writes, for each Service, slices listing matching Pods\' addresses with their conditions:\n\n```\n$ kubectl -n beacon get endpointslices -l kubernetes.io/service-name=beacon-api\nNAME               ADDRESSTYPE   PORTS   ENDPOINTS                 AGE\nbeacon-api-x7k2q   IPv4          8080    10.244.0.14,10.244.0.15   3h\n```\n\n`PORTS` shows the resolved target port — the number, after any name lookup — which is the quickest check that `targetPort` points where you think. `ENDPOINTS` lists only Pods whose Ready condition is true; a Pod failing its readiness probe is still in the slice but with `conditions.ready: false` (visible in `-o yaml`) and is not sent traffic. `kubectl describe service beacon-api` summarises the same under `Endpoints:`. An empty list means the selector matched no ready Pod: wrong labels, a namespace mismatch, or all Pods unready. `kubectl get pods -l app=beacon-api -n beacon` run with the Service\'s exact selector is the discriminating check for the first two; the `READY` column covers the third. The older `Endpoints` object still exists for compatibility, but slices are what kube-proxy reads.' },
    { h: 'Tracing from name to response', body: 'From a throwaway Pod in the namespace (`kubectl -n beacon run probe --rm -it --image=registry.lab/tools/netshoot:2.1 -- sh`): `nslookup beacon-api` returns the ClusterIP; `curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}" http://beacon-api/healthz` returns `200`. Each failure mode has a distinct signature. `NXDOMAIN` or `server can\'t find`: the Service does not exist in that namespace, or CoreDNS is unhealthy (`kubectl -n kube-system get pods -l k8s-app=kube-dns`). The name resolves but `curl: (7) Failed to connect`: no endpoints — the kube-proxy rules for a Service with an empty slice reject the connection. `Connection refused` after a pause: an endpoint exists but nothing listens on the target port inside the Pod — a `targetPort` mismatch or Beacon bound to the wrong port. A timeout: a NetworkPolicy (M09) or a node-level problem.\n\nProof of the user path is the `200` from a real request through the Service name, not `kubectl get svc` showing a ClusterIP, and not the Pod answering on its own address — bypassing the Service tests the Pod, not the path.' },
    { h: 'Repairing the two classic mismatches', body: '**Selector mismatch**: the Deployment template labels `app: beacon-api`, the Service selects `app: beacon`. Slice empty, connection refused. Fix the Service selector (a Deployment selector is immutable and would require a delete-and-recreate); `kubectl apply` on the Service and the slice fills within a second. **targetPort mismatch**: the slice shows two endpoints and `PORTS 8000`, Beacon listens on 8080. Fix `targetPort` to the named port `http` so the number lives only in the Pod template. Both fixes are a diff on one object; both are verified by the slice contents changing and the `curl` through the Service name succeeding.\n\nWhat is not a fix: pointing clients at Pod addresses (they change), `type: NodePort` to "expose it properly" (it changes reach, not the mismatch), or removing the readiness probe to make endpoints appear (lesson 05: that sends traffic to Pods that cannot serve it).' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'Service exists, Pods are Running, nothing answers', body: '```\n$ kubectl -n beacon get pods -o wide\nNAME                          READY   STATUS    IP            NODE\nbeacon-api-7c9d8b5f4-k2x9p    1/1     Running   10.244.0.14   kind-worker\nbeacon-api-7c9d8b5f4-r7qzt    1/1     Running   10.244.0.15   kind-worker\n$ kubectl -n beacon run probe --rm -it --image=registry.lab/tools/netshoot:2.1 -- curl -s -m 3 http://beacon-api/healthz\ncurl: (7) Failed to connect to beacon-api port 80 after 2 ms: Couldn\'t connect to server\n$ kubectl -n beacon get endpointslices -l kubernetes.io/service-name=beacon-api\nNAME               ADDRESSTYPE   PORTS   ENDPOINTS   AGE\nbeacon-api-x7k2q   IPv4          <unset>  <unset>     12m\n$ kubectl -n beacon get svc beacon-api -o jsonpath="{.spec.selector}"\n{"app":"beacon"}\n$ kubectl -n beacon get pods -l app=beacon\nNo resources found in beacon namespace.\n```\n\n- Name resolves (the error names port 80 on the Service address), both Pods are Ready, so hypotheses narrow to the Service side. The slice is empty: the selector matched nothing. Running the Service\'s own selector as a Pod query returns nothing — the labels are `app: beacon-api`.\n- Smallest change: in `svc/beacon-api.yaml`, `selector: {app: beacon-api}`; `kubectl apply -f`. The slice now lists both addresses with `PORTS 8080`.\n- User path: the same `curl` through the Service name returns `ok` with `200`; a `POST /v1/status` returns `202`. The Pods never changed; the path did.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'If the Pods are Running the Service will reach them.', right: 'The Service needs its selector to match the Pod labels, the Pods to be Ready, and `targetPort` to be a port the container listens on. Each is checked separately; the EndpointSlice shows the first two.' },
    { wrong: '`port` and `targetPort` must be the same number.', right: '`port` is what clients dial on the Service; `targetPort` is the container port. Using a named port keeps the number in the Pod template only.' },
    { wrong: 'Testing the Pod address directly proves the Service works.', right: 'It proves the Pod serves. The user path goes through the Service name, DNS, kube-proxy rules and the slice; test that.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict the EndpointSlice and the client error when the Service selector matches no Pod.', a: 'The slice shows no endpoints (`<unset>`); a client gets `Failed to connect` on the Service address (connection rejected), not a DNS error.' },
    { q: 'The slice lists two endpoints with `PORTS 8000`; Beacon listens on 8080. What does a client see and what is the fix?', a: '`Connection refused` from a Pod; fix `targetPort` (ideally to the named port `http`) so it resolves to 8080.' },
    { q: 'Why is a Pod failing its readiness probe absent from traffic although it still appears in the slice YAML?', a: 'Its endpoint carries `conditions.ready: false`; kube-proxy only programs ready endpoints, so it receives no connections.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m08-04', moduleId: 'M08', title: 'Configuration: ConfigMaps, Secrets and environment', mins: 15,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m08-04'], prereqs: ['les-m08-02'], refs: ['T04', 'T02'],
  objective: 'Inject Beacon\'s configuration through ConfigMaps and Secrets as environment variables and mounted files, explain when a change is picked up, and diagnose `CreateContainerConfigError`, `ErrImagePull` and `ImagePullBackOff` from Pod events.',
  model: 'A **ConfigMap** is a named set of key/value strings; a **Secret** is the same shape, base64-encoded in the API and treated differently by access control and by the kubelet (held in memory on the node) — it is **not** encrypted by being a Secret. A Pod consumes either as **environment variables** (fixed at container start) or as a **volume** of files (updated in place some time after the object changes, except through `subPath`). The kubelet resolves these references when it creates the container; a reference it cannot satisfy — a missing key, a missing object — stops the container before it starts, with an event that names the key. The same kubelet pulls the image, and an image it cannot fetch produces its own, different pair of states.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'ConfigMaps and Secrets as environment', body: '```\napiVersion: v1\nkind: ConfigMap\nmetadata: { name: beacon-config, namespace: beacon }\ndata:\n  BEACON_LISTEN: "0.0.0.0:8080"\n  BEACON_DB_HOST: "beacon-db"\n  BEACON_QUEUE_URL: "redis://beacon-queue:6379/0"\n---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Secret\nmetadata: { name: beacon-db, namespace: beacon }\ntype: Opaque\nstringData:\n  password: "<generated in the lab, never committed>"\n```\n\nIn the Pod template, `envFrom: [{configMapRef: {name: beacon-config}}]` imports every key as a variable; `env: [{name: BEACON_DB_PASSWORD, valueFrom: {secretKeyRef: {name: beacon-db, key: password}}}]` imports one key under a chosen name. `stringData` lets you write the plain value and the API stores it base64-encoded under `data`; `kubectl get secret beacon-db -o jsonpath="{.data.password}" | base64 -d` reads it back, which is why Secret access is an RBAC decision (M09) and the real protection is encryption at rest in etcd plus restricted read access, not the encoding. Environment variables are read once when the container starts: editing the ConfigMap changes nothing in running Pods. `kubectl rollout restart deployment/beacon-api` creates new Pods that read the new values — a rollout, with the same readiness gating.' },
    { h: 'Mounting as files, and when they update', body: 'For configuration that is a file — a YAML, a certificate — mount the object as a volume:\n\n```\nvolumes:\n  - name: config\n    configMap: { name: beacon-config-file }\ncontainers:\n  - name: api\n    volumeMounts:\n      - { name: config, mountPath: /app/config, readOnly: true }\n```\n\nEach key becomes a file under `/app/config`. The kubelet refreshes mounted ConfigMap and Secret volumes periodically (on the order of a minute by default) by swapping a symlink, so a process that re-reads the file sees the change without a restart — but one that read it at startup does not, and a mount using `subPath` to place a single file is never refreshed. Beacon reads `config/beacon.yaml` at start, so in practice both styles need a rollout restart for a change to take effect; the lab makes you prove which one you have.' },
    { h: 'Missing keys: CreateContainerConfigError', body: 'A reference the kubelet cannot resolve stops the container before it runs:\n\n```\n$ kubectl -n beacon get pods\nNAME                          READY   STATUS                       RESTARTS   AGE\nbeacon-api-5f6b7d9c8-m4wq2    0/1     CreateContainerConfigError   0          45s\n$ kubectl -n beacon describe pod beacon-api-5f6b7d9c8-m4wq2 | grep -A2 "Warning"\n  Warning  Failed  12s (x4 over 45s)  kubelet  Error: couldn\'t find key BEACON_QUEUE_URL in ConfigMap beacon/beacon-config\n```\n\nThe event names the object and the key. The usual causes are a key typo (`BEACON_QUEUE_URL` versus `BEACON_QUEUE`), the ConfigMap applied to the wrong namespace, or a Deployment rolled before its ConfigMap. The fix is the object the event names; the kubelet retries on its own and the Pod moves to `Running` once the key exists. `optional: true` on the reference makes a missing key or object non-fatal, which is right only for genuinely optional settings. Note the difference from lesson 02\'s `KeyError` crash: there the variable was absent from the Pod spec entirely, so the container started and Beacon failed; here the spec references a key that does not exist, so the container never starts.' },
    { h: 'Images: ErrImagePull and ImagePullBackOff', body: 'The kubelet pulls the image before anything else. A failure shows first as `ErrImagePull`, then — as the kubelet backs off between attempts — as `ImagePullBackOff`:\n\n```\n  Warning  Failed   30s  kubelet  Failed to pull image "registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.4": rpc error: ... manifest unknown\n  Warning  Failed   30s  kubelet  Error: ErrImagePull\n  Normal   BackOff  15s  kubelet  Back-off pulling image "registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.4"\n  Warning  Failed   15s  kubelet  Error: ImagePullBackOff\n```\n\nThe message distinguishes the causes: `manifest unknown` or `not found` is a tag that does not exist (a typo, or an image never pushed); `unauthorized` or `authentication required` is a private registry without credentials — fixed with a `kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson` Secret referenced by `imagePullSecrets` on the Pod (or the service account), never by making the registry public; a timeout or `no such host` is the node\'s network path to `registry.lab`. On kind, an image built locally must be loaded into the nodes (`kind load docker-image registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.2`) or pushed to a registry the nodes can reach; `imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent` then uses the loaded copy. Nothing about this is a scheduling problem, and the Pod is already on a node — the events say `kubelet`.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'Two Pods, two different "not starting" states', body: 'After rolling 1.4.3 with a new ConfigMap:\n\n```\n$ kubectl -n beacon get pods\nNAME                          READY   STATUS                       RESTARTS   AGE\nbeacon-api-5f6b7d9c8-m4wq2    0/1     CreateContainerConfigError   0          2m\nbeacon-worker-8d7c6b5a4-p9x1  0/1     ImagePullBackOff             0          2m\n$ kubectl -n beacon get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | tail -2\n2m  Warning  Failed  pod/beacon-api-5f6b7d9c8-m4wq2    Error: couldn\'t find key BEACON_QUEUE_URL in ConfigMap beacon/beacon-config\n2m  Warning  Failed  pod/beacon-worker-8d7c6b5a4-p9x1  Failed to pull image "registry.lab/beacon/worker:1.4.3": ... manifest unknown\n```\n\n- API: `kubectl -n beacon get configmap beacon-config -o jsonpath="{.data}"` shows the key spelled `BEACON_QUEUE`. Fix the ConfigMap key (the Deployment\'s reference is the intended name); apply; the kubelet retries and the Pod is `Running 1/1` within a minute with no rollout needed.\n- Worker: `docker buildx imagetools inspect registry.lab/beacon/worker:1.4.3` → `manifest unknown`. The worker image for 1.4.3 was never pushed; the pipeline built only the API. Push it (by digest, lesson M07-05), and the kubelet\'s next back-off attempt succeeds; the Pod moves `Pulling` → `Pulled` → `Running`.\n\nNeither Pod needed deleting; both loops retried once their inputs existed. Verification: `kubectl rollout status` for both Deployments, then a `POST /v1/status` through the Service and a worker log line showing it consumed the record.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'Editing a ConfigMap updates the running Pods\' environment.', right: 'Environment variables are read at container start. A rollout restart creates Pods that read the new values; mounted volumes refresh, but only for processes that re-read the file.' },
    { wrong: 'A Secret is encrypted, so it is safe to commit the manifest.', right: 'It is base64-encoded, which is reversible. Protection comes from RBAC and encryption at rest; generated values stay out of Git.' },
    { wrong: '`ImagePullBackOff` means the cluster is out of capacity or the image is too big.', right: 'It means the kubelet\'s pull failed and it is backing off between retries. The event message says why: missing tag, credentials, or network path to the registry.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict the Pod STATUS and event when the Deployment references `configMapKeyRef: {name: beacon-config, key: DB_HOST}` and the ConfigMap has `BEACON_DB_HOST`.', a: '`CreateContainerConfigError` with event `couldn\'t find key DB_HOST in ConfigMap beacon/beacon-config`; the container never starts.' },
    { q: 'An image pull event says `unauthorized: authentication required`. What is the fix and what is not?', a: 'An `imagePullSecrets` reference to a docker-registry Secret for `registry.lab`; not making the registry anonymous, not changing the tag.' },
    { q: 'After changing a ConfigMap used via `envFrom`, what single command makes Beacon pick it up safely?', a: '`kubectl rollout restart deployment/beacon-api`: new Pods read the new environment under the usual readiness-gated rollout.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m08-05', moduleId: 'M08', title: 'Probes: startup, readiness and liveness', mins: 16,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m08-05', 'obj-m08-03'], prereqs: ['les-m08-03', 'les-m08-04', 'les-m07-04'], refs: ['T22', 'T02'],
  objective: 'Configure startup, readiness and liveness probes for Beacon with deliberate thresholds, predict what a failure of each does to the Pod and to the Service\'s endpoints, diagnose a readiness failure from `kubectl describe`, and explain why removing a probe hides a failure rather than fixing it.',
  model: 'The kubelet runs three kinds of **probe** against a container. A **startup** probe gates the other two: until it succeeds, neither runs, which gives a slow starter time without lowering the bar afterwards. A **readiness** probe answers "should this Pod receive traffic?" — failing it sets the Pod\'s Ready condition false and removes it from Service endpoints, and does **not** restart anything; it is reversible the moment the probe passes again. A **liveness** probe answers "is this process beyond recovery?" — failing it makes the kubelet kill the container and start it again, counting a restart. Each probe has `periodSeconds`, `timeoutSeconds`, `failureThreshold` and `successThreshold`, and the product of period and threshold is how long a condition persists before the action. A probe is the contract between Beacon and the cluster; deleting it does not make Beacon healthy, it makes the cluster stop asking.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'The three probes on Beacon, with reasons for each number', body: '```\ncontainers:\n  - name: api\n    image: registry.lab/beacon/api:1.4.3\n    ports: [{ name: http, containerPort: 8080 }]\n    startupProbe:\n      httpGet: { path: /healthz, port: http }\n      periodSeconds: 2\n      failureThreshold: 30        # up to 60 s to start\n    readinessProbe:\n      httpGet: { path: /readyz, port: http }\n      periodSeconds: 5\n      failureThreshold: 3         # out of endpoints after ~15 s\n      successThreshold: 1\n    livenessProbe:\n      httpGet: { path: /healthz, port: http }\n      periodSeconds: 10\n      timeoutSeconds: 2\n      failureThreshold: 3         # restart after ~30 s of no answer\n```\n\n`/healthz` answers `200` if the process can serve a request at all — it checks nothing external. `/readyz` answers `200` only if Beacon can do its job right now: it has a database connection and its queue client is up. Readiness may depend on dependencies; liveness must not, or a database outage restarts every API Pod in a loop and turns one failure into two. The startup probe uses the cheap endpoint with a generous budget, so the liveness probe can stay strict without killing a Pod that is still loading. Defaults when unset: `periodSeconds: 10`, `timeoutSeconds: 1`, `failureThreshold: 3`, `successThreshold: 1`; `initialDelaySeconds` is the old way to wait for startup, and the startup probe replaces it. `tcpSocket`, `exec` and `grpc` probes exist for processes that do not speak HTTP.' },
    { h: 'What each failure does, precisely', body: '**Startup failing**: the container keeps running; readiness and liveness are not evaluated; after `failureThreshold × periodSeconds` of failures the kubelet kills and restarts the container (it is treated like liveness). Events say `Startup probe failed`. **Readiness failing**: after `failureThreshold` consecutive failures the Pod condition `Ready` becomes `False`; `kubectl get pods` shows `0/1` with STATUS still `Running`; the EndpointSlice marks the endpoint `ready: false` and kube-proxy stops sending it connections; the Deployment\'s `readyReplicas` drops; **no restart**, `RESTARTS` stays at 0. When the probe passes `successThreshold` times, all of that reverses. **Liveness failing**: after the threshold the kubelet sends SIGTERM (then SIGKILL after the grace period), the container restarts under `restartPolicy`, `RESTARTS` increments, and events read `Liveness probe failed: ... Container api failed liveness probe, will be restarted`. Repeated liveness failures look like `CrashLoopBackOff` with a growing restart count and no crash in the logs.\n\nSo `Running 0/1, RESTARTS 0` plus `Readiness probe failed` events is readiness; `RESTARTS` climbing plus `Liveness probe failed` is liveness; and a Pod that never leaves `0/1` with `Startup probe failed` has not got past its startup budget.' },
    { h: 'Diagnosing a readiness failure', body: '```\n$ kubectl -n beacon get pods\nNAME                          READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE\nbeacon-api-5f6b7d9c8-m4wq2    0/1     Running   0          6m\nbeacon-api-5f6b7d9c8-t8n3v    0/1     Running   0          6m\n$ kubectl -n beacon describe pod beacon-api-5f6b7d9c8-m4wq2 | grep -E "Ready|Readiness"\n  Ready             False\n  ContainersReady   False\n  Warning  Unhealthy  10s (x70 over 6m)  kubelet  Readiness probe failed: HTTP probe failed with statuscode: 503\n$ kubectl -n beacon exec beacon-api-5f6b7d9c8-m4wq2 -- python -c "import urllib.request;print(urllib.request.urlopen(\'http://127.0.0.1:8080/readyz\').read())"\nurllib.error.HTTPError: HTTP Error 503: {\"db\": \"connection refused to beacon-db:5432\"}\n```\n\n- Both Pods Running, zero restarts, Ready False, probe returning 503: readiness, not liveness, and the process is fine. The Service slice shows both endpoints `ready: false`; the user path returns `Failed to connect` through the Service, which is the correct behaviour — Beacon cannot serve, so it is not offered.\n- The probe\'s body says why: the database. `kubectl -n beacon get pods -l app=beacon-db` → the database Pod is `Pending` after a node restart (lesson 01\'s scheduler path). Fix the database; within one readiness period both API Pods flip to `1/1`, the slice marks them ready, the user path returns `202`.\n\nThe tempting shortcut — delete the readiness probe so the Pods show `1/1` — would have put two Pods that answer `503` to every real request back into the Service and hidden the database outage behind an API that looks up.' },
    { h: 'Never remove a probe to hide a failure', body: 'A probe that fails is reporting a true statement about the process. Deleting the readiness probe changes what the cluster believes, not what Beacon can do; users then get the error directly instead of being routed to a Pod that works. Deleting the liveness probe leaves a wedged process running forever. Loosening thresholds to absurd values is the same move in slow motion. The course treats all three as failed attempts, like `chmod 777` in M01.\n\nLegitimate probe changes exist: a liveness probe that checks the database is wrong and should be narrowed to the process; a readiness period of 1 second with a 1 second timeout against an endpoint that takes 1.5 seconds under load flaps and should be relaxed with measurement; a startup budget that is shorter than a cold start should grow. Each of those is a change to match the probe to the process\'s real behaviour, made with evidence, and verified by the user path afterwards — not a change to make a red thing green.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'A liveness probe that made an outage worse', body: 'Beacon\'s first manifest had one probe, `livenessProbe: httpGet /readyz`, where `/readyz` checks the database. A planned PostgreSQL restart took 90 seconds. Timeline from events:\n\n```\n09:00:05  Warning  Unhealthy  Liveness probe failed: HTTP probe failed with statuscode: 503\n09:00:25  Normal   Killing    Container api failed liveness probe, will be restarted\n09:00:40  Normal   Started    Started container api\n09:01:10  Warning  Unhealthy  Liveness probe failed: HTTP probe failed with statuscode: 503\n09:01:30  Normal   Killing    Container api failed liveness probe, will be restarted\n```\n\nEvery API Pod restarted twice during a database outage it could have waited out, `RESTARTS` reached 2, and because there was no readiness probe the Pods were in the Service the whole time, so clients saw connection errors during restarts and 503s between them — two failure modes from one dependency.\n\nThe fix: `livenessProbe` on `/healthz` (process only), `readinessProbe` on `/readyz` (dependencies), a `startupProbe` with a 60 s budget. Re-running the same database restart: `RESTARTS 0`, both Pods `0/1` for the 90 seconds with `Readiness probe failed` events, endpoints empty, then back to `1/1` with no intervention. Clients saw a clean "no backend" instead of flapping Pods. The debrief question for S16 is this timeline: the symptom, why deleting the probe would have hidden the database outage, and the user-path check that proved the fix.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'A failing readiness probe restarts the container.', right: 'It only removes the Pod from Service endpoints and sets Ready false; `RESTARTS` does not change. Liveness is the probe that restarts.' },
    { wrong: 'Liveness should check the database so unhealthy Pods get replaced.', right: 'A dependency outage would then restart every Pod in a loop. Liveness checks the process; readiness checks whether it can serve now.' },
    { wrong: 'Removing a probe that keeps failing fixes the deployment.', right: 'It stops the cluster from noticing. The Pods still cannot serve; users now receive the failures directly. Fix the cause, or adjust the probe with evidence.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict `READY`, `RESTARTS` and endpoint membership for a Pod whose readiness probe has failed four times with `failureThreshold: 3`.', a: '`0/1`, restarts unchanged, endpoint marked `ready: false` and receiving no traffic; STATUS still `Running`.' },
    { q: 'What does a startup probe change about the liveness probe while the container is starting?', a: 'Liveness (and readiness) are not evaluated until the startup probe succeeds, so a slow start is not killed and the liveness probe can stay strict.' },
    { q: 'Events show `Liveness probe failed` and `RESTARTS 5` with no traceback in `kubectl logs --previous`. What is the probe doing to Beacon?', a: 'Killing a process that may be fine but cannot answer the probe in time (or is checking a dependency); fix the probe\'s target or timeout with measurement, not by deleting it.' }
  ]
});
